WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:11.800
- My name is Timothy Snyder

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:14.850
and I am here to welcome you

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.960
to the first in a series of lectures

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.570
which are organized within the framework

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:23.970
of a Franke seminar
here at Yale University.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:27.400
The seminar is run by Professor
Jason Stanley and myself.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.797
Its title is "Mass Incarceration
in the Soviet Union

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.160
and the United States."

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.590
We teach this as a regular Yale class

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.420
but we have the privilege and the pleasure

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.700
of being able to invite
people from whom we've learned

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.030
and people whom we admire

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:42.460
and people whose work
we're reading in the class

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:44.310
to give public lectures as well.

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.320
So I'll be introducing

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.910
and the students of the class are present

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:51.120
as well as others of you who have joined,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.420
and thank you for that.

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.700
I'm gonna briefly
introduce Anne Applebaum.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.900
She is known on the one side

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.260
as an author of a series
of extremely important

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.953
and lauded books on the
history of communism.

00:01:07.887 --> 00:01:10.120
Most recently there is a history

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:13.060
of the famine in Ukraine, "Red Famine,"

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.710
which has brought the academic debate

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:18.080
and her own interpretation

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:21.180
into a highly accessible form.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.220
I learned a lot from that book.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.330
A few years ago there was a book about

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:26.590
the post-war takeovers,

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.280
actually a slightly revisionist account

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.350
of the post-war communist
takeovers in Eastern Europe

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.450
under the title "Iron Curtain."

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:35.510
And then towards the
beginning of the century,

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.520
in 2003 to 2004,

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:42.008
Anne wrote the book for
which she won a big prize

00:01:42.008 --> 00:01:44.060
and which we read in the class

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.090
and which will be part
of her subject today.

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:50.060
It's called "Gulag: A History."

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:53.263
Clearly the most accessible
account of the Gulag

00:01:53.263 --> 00:01:56.460
as well as providing
not just documentation

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.530
but themes and interpretation.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.270
Anne is however also the author of a book

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:04.930
which in some sense
stays with me the post.

00:02:04.930 --> 00:02:06.470
It's called "Between East and West."

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:08.550
And those of you who are
students and thinking about

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.180
how you're going to spend
the rest of your life,

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:11.650
the book is a kind of meditation

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:14.120
among many other things on that.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:17.240
But in that book there is
this kind of foresight,

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.008
the book is a couple of
decades old now, more,

00:02:21.008 --> 00:02:23.430
a kind of advanced warning
that there are things

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:25.070
between Poland and Russia,

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:26.170
that there are these countries,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.377
this Belarus, this Ukraine,

00:02:27.377 --> 00:02:29.360
and that someday they're gonna matter.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.417
And there are a number of
other things in the book.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:35.220
There's an interesting
reference to antisemitism

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.880
and the future of the Jewish
question in Eastern Europe.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.450
So, Anne has written an awful lot

00:02:40.450 --> 00:02:42.790
and from a number of different angles.

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:45.079
At a time when we're more
and more specialized,

00:02:45.079 --> 00:02:48.520
Anne has done two things
which are unusual.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.330
In writing about the communist world

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.800
she's also been a prominent
journalist about Europe

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:54.830
and the United States,

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:56.530
catching many of the
most important stories,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.920
for example, of the Trump administration

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:00.370
right at the beginning.

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:04.360
And finally, she functions
not just in the United States

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:05.800
and not just in Europe,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:06.880
although that's already a lot,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.220
but from many different
international angles.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.670
And in these ways she is something
which is rare these days,

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:14.650
all too rare,

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:15.950
which is an intellectual.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.220
We're very glad to have Anne
introduce these lectures

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.060
and help get our course make a start.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.877
Again, the subject for lecture is

00:03:23.877 --> 00:03:27.540
"The Gulag: What We Now
Know and Why It Matters."

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.610
Anne, the floor is yours.

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.390
- Tim, thank you so much.

00:03:31.390 --> 00:03:36.032
I so enjoy these long introductions.

00:03:36.032 --> 00:03:36.865
(Tim laughs)

00:03:36.865 --> 00:03:41.800
You also play so many
different roles as well,

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.160
as does Jason,

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:45.170
and I've had a chance to look

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:46.480
at the syllabus for your course

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.740
and it's a very interesting one.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.200
I'm sorry I'm not taking it myself.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.240
You're really combining a
lot of different things.

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:56.850
This evening I'm gonna
talk a lot about something

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.520
that for Americans in this context

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.460
may sound a little different or strange,

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:06.407
and it's not merely the
history of the Gulag

00:04:06.407 --> 00:04:08.730
but the legacy and memory of the Gulag.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.230
How does it continue
to matter or not matter

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:13.830
in Russian politics today.

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.100
And actually as I was thinking
about speaking this evening

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.400
I realized that I'm
talking to a generation

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.140
that might not know

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:23.450
that the Gulag for many decades

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:27.190
was a scarcely known,
hardly understood subject.

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.500
It was a vast system of camps

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:32.120
tucked far away in the Soviet Union.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:35.950
Most Americans, most Europeans
knew very little about it.

00:04:35.950 --> 00:04:38.550
Well, I'll speak about
that a little bit more.

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:43.550
And it only really came to
light in its full, you know,

00:04:44.710 --> 00:04:47.020
we really began to understand what it was

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.460
and exactly how big it was

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.730
after Soviet archives
began to open in the 1990s.

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:55.670
And I was extremely
lucky to be able to work

00:04:55.670 --> 00:04:57.580
in the Soviet Union in the 1990s,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:58.670
that's a whole other subject

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.660
which if some of you
wanna discuss afterwards

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.430
I'm happy to talk about,

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:06.729
and my book was based on in some cases

00:05:06.729 --> 00:05:10.040
the first plans and documents
that nobody had seen

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.283
or nobody had seen outside
of Russia for a long time.

00:05:14.186 --> 00:05:17.490
And it was really in the
process of doing that,

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.150
in the process of going
through these documents,

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:24.370
that we began to understand
really what the Gulag was.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:27.680
And so although the
legacy of Stalin's crimes

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.640
and the Gulag itself

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.950
will be the ultimate subject of my talk,

00:05:30.950 --> 00:05:33.500
I will begin with a brief account

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.680
of what it is that we know now,

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.870
so what did we learn about the camps

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.130
since the breakup of the Soviet Union.

00:05:40.130 --> 00:05:43.540
You know, what we know now that
we didn't know 30 years ago.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:46.515
Obviously, in writing
my history of the Gulag

00:05:46.515 --> 00:05:49.710
I didn't claim to have
discovered a brand new topic

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:52.790
that had never been touched on before.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:55.887
Some of you will know
Solzhenitsyn's famous book

00:05:55.887 --> 00:05:57.170
"The Gulag Archipelago"

00:05:57.170 --> 00:06:01.320
which was a kind of oral
history of the camp system

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.310
that he published in the
West as a piece of samizdat,

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:06.130
meaning it was not published
ever in the Soviet Union

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:09.810
but it was published outside
of the USSR in the 1970s.

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:13.200
Although that was a document
based on his own experiences

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.730
and on the experiences of other people,

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.250
he didn't have archives,

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:20.770
actually he got the outline
of the story largely right.

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:22.490
He didn't have archives

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:24.840
but he did understand the basic outlines

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.350
of the Gulag's history

00:06:26.350 --> 00:06:28.220
from its earliest incarnation

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.930
on the Solovetsky Islands in the White Sea

00:06:30.930 --> 00:06:34.440
and how it spread across
the Far North from there

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.863
and then across the country.

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.500
But as a kind of asterisk,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.980
in the years that I
spent researching my book

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:45.650
I conclude that archives
really do make a difference

00:06:45.650 --> 00:06:47.490
in how we understand Soviet history,

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:51.120
and so I was able to work in
archives in Moscow, in Karelia.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:54.010
I had access to documents from
archives in Saint Petersburg,

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.653
Perm, Vorkuta, Kolyma, Novosibirsk.

00:06:58.243 --> 00:07:00.700
At one point I was handed
a part of the archive

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.740
of a small camp called
Kedrovy Shor in the Far North

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:07.010
and I was politely asked
if I wanted to buy it,

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:09.763
which I did and it's now
at the Hoover Institute.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:13.610
What was available to me
was often quite ordinary.

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:16.410
It was the day-to-day archive
of the Gulag administration,

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:19.360
for example, with inspector
reports and financial accounts

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:22.180
and letters from the
camp directors to Moscow.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.540
But it's true that when reading
these kinds of documents

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:26.530
the full extent of the system

00:07:26.530 --> 00:07:29.840
and its importance to the Soviet economy

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.550
came sharply into focus.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:34.380
So, for example,

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.340
these documents enabled
me to be far more precise

00:07:37.340 --> 00:07:39.150
than it had ever been
possible in the past,

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.740
and so thanks to archives
we were able to learn

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.740
that there were at least
476 different camp systems

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.290
in the Soviet era,

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.750
each one made up of
hundreds or even thousands

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.050
of individual camps or (mumbles).

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:56.430
So the camps were,

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.900
there would be a central camp

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:00.707
and then there would be kind of (mumbles),

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:03.560
and this was particularly
true of the forestry camps.

00:08:03.560 --> 00:08:04.720
So there would be one camp

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.450
and then there would
be small forestry camps

00:08:06.450 --> 00:08:09.080
all up and down a highway usually.

00:08:09.080 --> 00:08:10.690
And sometimes they were spread out

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:15.220
over thousands of square miles
of otherwise empty tundra.

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.880
We also know now that the vast
majority of people in them,

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.090
prisoners, were peasants and workers

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:22.630
and not the intellectuals who later wrote

00:08:22.630 --> 00:08:24.180
the famous memoirs and books.

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:25.070
There were some of them too

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.460
but they were smaller in numbers.

00:08:27.460 --> 00:08:29.700
We also know that with a few exceptions

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:32.340
the camps were not constructed explicitly

00:08:32.340 --> 00:08:34.240
in order to kill people.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:36.760
Stalin preferred to use firing squads

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:38.530
to conduct his mass executions.

00:08:38.530 --> 00:08:41.433
He did kill people en
mass but not in camps.

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:45.900
Nevertheless, at times
they were very lethal.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:48.900
So nearly 1/4 of the
Gulag's prisoners died

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.390
during the war years, for example,

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:53.113
between 1941 and 1945.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:55.740
They were also very fluid.

00:08:55.740 --> 00:08:59.400
So prisoners left because they
died, because they escaped,

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.500
because they had short sentences,

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:03.760
because they were being
released into the Red Army

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.420
or because they had been
promoted from prisoner to guard

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.340
which sometimes happened.

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:10.180
There were also frequent amnesties

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:12.490
for the old and for the ill,

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.500
for pregnant women and anyone
else who was no longer useful

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:17.810
to the forest labor system.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:20.090
But these releases were
invariably followed

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:21.620
by new waves of arrests,

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:25.370
so it was a fluid
system, it wasn't static,

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:27.419
people's sentences could change,

00:09:27.419 --> 00:09:29.510
either be much lengthened
or much shortened

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:31.450
even as they were there.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.553
It wasn't a rule-of-law-based system.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:36.520
People weren't sentenced by real courts,

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:37.700
they didn't have real sentences.

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:40.000
I mean, sometimes they had
these kind of kangaroo courts,

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.000
they were called troikas.

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:46.110
But the camp population actually changed

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.770
according to the Soviet
Union's labor needs.

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.860
But as a result of this, between 1929,

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:56.710
which is when they first
became a mass phenomenon,

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:59.600
and 1953 which is the
year of Stalin's death,

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:03.180
some 18 million people
passed through them.

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:06.150
In addition, a further six
or seven million people

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:10.290
were deported not to camps
but to exile villages.

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:13.440
And so in total that means
that the number of people

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.770
with some experience of
incarceration or imprisonment

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:18.700
in Stalin's Soviet Union

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.500
could have run as high as 25 million,

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.793
which at that time was
about 15% of the population.

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:32.210
We also know where these
camps were, namely everywhere.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:34.807
And so although some of
you who know Solzhenitsyn

00:10:34.807 --> 00:10:36.760
and who know Soviet literature

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:38.950
might be familiar with the image

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:40.780
of the prisoner in the snowstorm

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.640
digging coal with a pickax,

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:44.660
there were camps in central Moscow

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:46.830
where prisoners built apartment blocks

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:48.580
or designed airplanes,

00:10:48.580 --> 00:10:50.460
there were camps in Krasnoyarsk

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:54.210
where prisoners ran nuclear power plants,

00:10:54.210 --> 00:10:57.290
there were fishing camps
on the Pacific Coast.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:00.360
I once had the chance to look
at the Gulag photo albums,

00:11:00.360 --> 00:11:03.910
these were albums put together
by the Gulag administrators,

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:05.770
and they included pictures of prisoners

00:11:05.770 --> 00:11:08.120
with their camels in Central Asia.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:10.990
And so from Akhtubinsk to Yakutsk

00:11:10.990 --> 00:11:14.270
there was not really a
single major population

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:15.160
in the Soviet Union

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.340
that did not have its
own local camp or camps

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:23.710
and not a single industry
that did not employ prisoners.

00:11:23.710 --> 00:11:27.300
So over the years prisoners
built roads and railroads,

00:11:27.300 --> 00:11:29.350
power plants and chemical factories,

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:32.650
manufactured weapons, furniture,

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:34.730
even children's toys.

00:11:34.730 --> 00:11:37.230
In the Soviet Union of the 1940s,

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:38.380
which was in fact the decade

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:40.360
that the camps reached their zenith,

00:11:40.360 --> 00:11:42.870
it would have been very
difficult in many places

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:46.330
to go about your daily business
and not run into prisoners,

00:11:46.330 --> 00:11:49.050
and it is therefore no
longer possible to argue

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:50.350
as some once did

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:51.990
that the camps were known to only

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:55.208
a small proportion of the population.

00:11:55.208 --> 00:11:58.470
We also understand better
the chronology of the camps.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:03.470
So we've long known that Lenin
built the first ones in 1918,

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:06.240
so at the time of the
Bolshevik Revolution,

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:09.300
so the camps absolutely date
back to the very beginning.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.730
And originally they were kind
of ad hoc emergency measure

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:15.430
to contain enemies of the people,

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:18.200
which was a designation that meant

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:20.631
enemies of the revolution,
people we don't like,

00:12:20.631 --> 00:12:23.100
it could be applied to almost anybody.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:26.180
Again, this is not a system
that has rule of law.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:27.490
And of course they were designed

00:12:27.490 --> 00:12:29.050
to prevent counterrevolution.

00:12:29.050 --> 00:12:32.210
They also were sometimes meant
to reeducate the bourgeoisie,

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:33.810
the opponents of the Bolsheviks.

00:12:34.840 --> 00:12:37.980
We also have long known that
Stalin and his successors,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:42.560
I mean, excuse me, that Lenin
and his immediate successors

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:45.280
expanded the camps to
the Solovetsky Islands.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.240
It's a group of islands in the
Far North in the White Sea,

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:51.650
in the northern part of
Russia, in the 1920s.

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:54.110
And that original expansion was, again,

00:12:54.110 --> 00:12:58.030
initially for the political
opponents of the Bolsheviks

00:12:58.030 --> 00:12:59.680
who were often other socialists,

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:02.410
so social democrats or
social revolutionaries,

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:03.760
people who the Bolsheviks

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.120
found to be ideologically threatening,

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.810
and they were initially put
in this group of camps on a,

00:13:08.810 --> 00:13:11.443
there was a monastery on
the islands in the north,

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:17.213
although that was initially
a relatively small operation.

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.040
But the archives also help us understand

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:24.480
why Stalin chose to expand them in 1929.

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:27.840
So in that year Stalin
launched the five-year plan.

00:13:27.840 --> 00:13:30.240
This was an extraordinarily
costly attempt,

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:33.780
costly in both human lives
and natural resources,

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:36.590
to force a 20% annual increase

00:13:36.590 --> 00:13:38.860
in the Soviet Union's industrial output

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.270
and to collect agriculture.

00:13:40.270 --> 00:13:43.860
This was Stalin's attempt to
push the Soviet Union faster,

00:13:43.860 --> 00:13:45.560
to modernize faster.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:47.530
And one of the side effects of the plan

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:48.840
was millions of arrests

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:50.870
as peasants were forced off their land,

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:52.960
maybe you'll discuss
that later in the course,

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:55.740
and imprisoned if they refused.

00:13:55.740 --> 00:13:58.660
It also led to an enormous labor shortage.

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:00.360
Suddenly, the Soviet Union found itself

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.070
in need of coal, gas, and minerals,

00:14:03.070 --> 00:14:04.810
most of which could be found only

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:08.483
in the Far North of the country.

00:14:09.590 --> 00:14:12.210
Following a series of discussions

00:14:12.210 --> 00:14:14.850
which used almost exclusively
economic language.

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:17.090
Again, I had the chance to read

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:19.880
as the leaders of the Soviet Union,

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:20.990
this is after Lenin's death,

00:14:20.990 --> 00:14:23.210
this is now when Stalin is a leader,

00:14:23.210 --> 00:14:26.983
as they were discussing
how to expand the camps.

00:14:28.177 --> 00:14:31.110
There are records of the conversations

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.890
that the Politburo had at that time,

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:34.440
the leadership of the Soviet Union,

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.990
and what's very striking about them

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:39.360
is that almost exclusively
economic language is being used.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:42.330
So the decision is taken
that we need the prisoners

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:43.883
to extract the minerals.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:46.870
And of course to the secret policemen

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:48.350
who were charged with carrying out

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:49.910
the construction of the camps

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:51.970
this made a great deal of sense.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:54.130
So here is how Alexei Loginov,

00:14:54.130 --> 00:14:57.180
who's the former deputy
commander of the Norilsk camps,

00:14:57.180 --> 00:14:59.120
these were north of the Arctic Circle,

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:03.580
this is how he justified
the use of prisoner labor

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.250
in a 1992 interview, so many years later.

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.147
He said, "If we had sent civilians,

00:15:09.147 --> 00:15:10.997
"we would first have had to build houses

00:15:10.997 --> 00:15:12.037
"for them to live in.

00:15:12.037 --> 00:15:14.227
"And how could civilians live there?

00:15:14.227 --> 00:15:16.167
"With prisoners it is easy.

00:15:16.167 --> 00:15:18.957
"All you need is a barrack,
a stove with a chimney,

00:15:18.957 --> 00:15:20.620
"and they survive."

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:23.610
So prisoners in Gulag
documents are often referred to

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:26.290
as (speaks in foreign
language), contingence.

00:15:26.290 --> 00:15:28.520
From the point of view
of the Soviet leadership

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.050
and of camp commanders,

00:15:30.050 --> 00:15:32.350
they were an ingredient of production.

00:15:32.350 --> 00:15:35.490
They were like lumps of
coal or bars of steel.

00:15:35.490 --> 00:15:38.250
Enemies of the people didn't
need to live like civilians

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:39.960
in normal houses.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.270
They could be put in barracks,

00:15:41.270 --> 00:15:42.770
it didn't really matter
what happened to them,

00:15:42.770 --> 00:15:44.380
if there wasn't quite enough food for them

00:15:44.380 --> 00:15:46.270
it wasn't a tragedy,

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:48.390
they were both cheap and expendable.

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:50.470
They weren't human, they were enemies,

00:15:50.470 --> 00:15:51.690
they could be used in this way

00:15:51.690 --> 00:15:53.250
which was very useful

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.023
for a country that wanted
to modernize very fast.

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:59.120
That isn't to say that the camps,

00:15:59.120 --> 00:16:01.300
in addition to their economic function,

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:03.930
which as I say that's how
they were always described,

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:06.340
were not also intended to terrorize

00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:08.920
and subjugate the population.

00:16:08.920 --> 00:16:10.760
Certainly, prison in camp regimes

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:13.120
that were dictated in
minute detail by Moscow

00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:14.990
were very openly designed to humiliate

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:17.000
and dehumanize prisoners.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:19.600
So the prisoners' belts
and buttons and garters

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:22.040
and items made of plastic
were taken away from them.

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:24.110
Again, they were described as enemies.

00:16:24.110 --> 00:16:26.530
They were forbidden to
use the word comrades

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:28.940
which was only for people, you know,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.620
for fully paid-up citizens of the state.

00:16:31.620 --> 00:16:33.950
And, again, those kinds
of measures contributed

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:36.820
to their dehumanization

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:38.930
in the eyes of guards and bureaucrats

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:41.190
who therefore found it that much easier

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.020
not to treat them as fellow citizens

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.180
or even as fellow human beings.

00:16:45.180 --> 00:16:46.810
And in fact this turned out to be

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:49.280
quite a powerful ideological combination.

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:51.510
So the disregarding of
the humanity of prisoners,

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:52.680
on the one hand,

00:16:52.680 --> 00:16:56.100
combined with the overwhelming
need to fulfill the plan.

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:57.720
And nowhere is this clearer

00:16:57.720 --> 00:16:59.610
than in the camp inspection reports.

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:02.700
These were submitted periodically
by local prosecutors,

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:05.300
kept neatly on file in Moscow,

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.180
which is where I found them.

00:17:07.180 --> 00:17:08.420
When I first began to read them

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:10.170
I was actually quite shocked at first

00:17:10.170 --> 00:17:11.600
both by their frankness

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:14.510
and the peculiar kind
of outrage they express.

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.920
So, for example, describing
conditions in Vorkutlag,

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:21.090
this was a railroad
construction camp in Tatarstan,

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:23.440
in July 1942,

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:25.337
one inspector complained that

00:17:25.337 --> 00:17:28.327
"the whole population of the
camp, including free workers,

00:17:28.327 --> 00:17:29.827
"lives off flour.

00:17:29.827 --> 00:17:32.357
"The only meal for
prisoners is so-called bread

00:17:32.357 --> 00:17:35.740
"baked with flour and water
without meats or fats."

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:38.480
And as a result, the
inspector went on indignantly,

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:41.420
there was a high rate of
illness, particularly scurvy,

00:17:41.420 --> 00:17:44.710
and not surprisingly the
camp was failing to meet

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.460
its production norms.

00:17:46.460 --> 00:17:49.750
So this outrage, you're
mistreating these prisoners,

00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:52.260
this ceased to seem surprising

00:17:52.260 --> 00:17:55.450
after I had read several
dozens similar reports,

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:57.240
each one of which used more or less

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:58.660
the same sort of language

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.710
and each of which ended with more or less

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:02.220
the same ritual conclusion.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.890
You know, conditions need to be improved

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:05.730
so that the prisoners will work harder

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:08.340
and so that the production
norms will be met.

00:18:08.340 --> 00:18:10.780
But very little was actually done.

00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:12.560
So while it might have been expected

00:18:12.560 --> 00:18:14.020
that living conditions in the Gulag

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:15.710
were poor during the war,

00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:17.799
as they were all across the Soviet Union,

00:18:17.799 --> 00:18:20.500
a nationwide inspection, for example,

00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:22.760
of 23 large camps in 1948

00:18:22.760 --> 00:18:25.110
still concluded among other things

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:29.360
that 75% of the prisoners
in Norillag, in Norilsk,

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.150
had no warm boots,

00:18:31.150 --> 00:18:32.420
that the number of prisoners

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.460
unfit for hard labor in Karelia,

00:18:34.460 --> 00:18:35.850
meaning they were too weak,

00:18:35.850 --> 00:18:37.360
had recently tripled,

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:39.447
that death rates were
still, quote unquote,

00:18:39.447 --> 00:18:41.900
"too high" in 1/2 a dozen camps,

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:43.710
meaning that it's too high to allow

00:18:43.710 --> 00:18:45.213
for efficient production.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.660
Without question the
expansion of the camps

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.440
distorted the Soviet economy.

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:52.540
With so much cheap labor available

00:18:52.540 --> 00:18:55.120
the Soviet economy took far
longer than it should have done

00:18:55.120 --> 00:18:56.510
to become mechanized.

00:18:56.510 --> 00:19:01.210
This is of course the irony of
Stalin's modernization push.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:04.270
Problems were solved by
calling for more workers.

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:05.750
Famously, the White Sea Canal,

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:07.890
one of the early Gulag projects,

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.290
was built with pickaxes
even at a time when

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:14.320
more sophisticated
equipment was available.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:19.090
With so many poorly trained
people working under coercion,

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:22.630
the construction was not of
the highest quality either.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:27.080
By one account, labor or
productivity among the free workers

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:28.410
in the forestry industry

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.300
was nearly three times that of prisoners

00:19:32.674 --> 00:19:35.140
working in the forestry camps.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:37.210
But the camps also distorted the way

00:19:37.210 --> 00:19:39.700
that people in the lands
of the former Soviet Union

00:19:39.700 --> 00:19:40.740
thought about economics.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:43.840
This is a little diversion
but it's an interesting point

00:19:43.840 --> 00:19:45.840
which I can illustrate
by describing a trip

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:47.350
that I took a few years ago,

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:48.750
quite a few years ago now,

00:19:48.750 --> 00:19:51.603
to the city of Vorkuta
on the Arctic Circle.

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:54.880
Vorkuta's history begins in 1931

00:19:54.880 --> 00:19:56.380
which is when a group of colonists

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:58.440
first arrived in the region by boat

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:00.590
up the northern waterways.

00:20:00.590 --> 00:20:02.790
Although even the czars had
known about the region's

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.530
enormous coal reserves,

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:06.160
no one had managed to work out

00:20:06.160 --> 00:20:08.850
precisely how to get the
coal out of the ground

00:20:08.850 --> 00:20:10.500
given the real horror of life

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:13.330
in a place where
temperatures regularly drop

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:16.210
to minus 30 or minus 40 in the winter,

00:20:16.210 --> 00:20:18.670
where the sun doesn't shine
for six months of the year,

00:20:18.670 --> 00:20:21.720
and where in the summer,
which is when I was there,

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:26.720
the mosquitoes travel in
these enormous dark clouds.

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.690
But Stalin found a way

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:34.040
by making use of another
sort of vast reserve.

00:20:34.040 --> 00:20:37.600
Vorkuta's original 23
settlers were prisoners

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:39.910
and the leaders of that
founding expedition

00:20:39.910 --> 00:20:40.880
were secret policemen,

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:43.520
and over the subsequent 2 1/2 decades

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:46.300
a million more prisoners
passed through Vorkuta,

00:20:46.300 --> 00:20:50.130
one of the two or three most
notorious hubs of the Gulag.

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:51.750
With the help of prisoners,

00:20:51.750 --> 00:20:53.470
the Soviet authorities built shops

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:55.443
and swimming pools and schools.

00:20:56.430 --> 00:20:58.060
But the cost of heating

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:00.490
these shoddy Soviet apartment blocks

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:01.760
for 11 months of the year

00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:03.690
turned out to be astronomical,

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:06.840
far more than the value
of the coal itself.

00:21:06.840 --> 00:21:08.410
The city's infrastructure,

00:21:08.410 --> 00:21:12.000
which was built on this
constantly shifting permafrost,

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:15.070
required huge efforts to maintain.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.490
Instead, miners could have flow in

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:18.950
on two or three-week shifts

00:21:18.950 --> 00:21:22.747
like they do in Alaska or in Canada.

00:21:22.747 --> 00:21:26.560
There was no need to
build this city there.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.870
Nevertheless, the city
of Vorkuta kept going

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:32.240
throughout the 1970s and 1980s and 1990s

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:35.293
and many thousands of people
still live there today.

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:39.050
Truth, of course, is that
Vorkuta is unnecessary,

00:21:39.050 --> 00:21:40.540
the city is unnecessary.

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:44.450
Why build kindergartens and
an university in the tundra?

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:46.320
Why build a puppet theater?

00:21:46.320 --> 00:21:47.523
Vorkuta had three.

00:21:48.700 --> 00:21:51.550
But in Vorkuta it was really
difficult to ask such questions

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.470
even after the USSR collapsed,

00:21:53.470 --> 00:21:55.420
and I have a particularly strong memory

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:58.520
of a day that I spent there
with a retired geologist.

00:21:58.520 --> 00:21:59.920
She was named Genia.

00:21:59.920 --> 00:22:01.490
And together we walked around the city,

00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:03.130
we went to the prisoners cemeteries,

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:06.090
we went around the ruins of
the Geological Institute,

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:08.920
a once solid structure
complete with a column,

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:12.810
a Stalinist portico and a
red star on the pediment.

00:22:12.810 --> 00:22:16.430
Genia's Polish parents had been
arrested and deported there

00:22:16.430 --> 00:22:17.800
in the 1940s

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:19.920
and she knew that story, of course,

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:22.140
and she knew the city's history very well.

00:22:22.140 --> 00:22:25.930
And nevertheless she railed against

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:27.900
the people she called
the greedy bureaucrats

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:29.990
who had shut down the institute

00:22:29.990 --> 00:22:32.890
and who were shutting down
those parts of the city

00:22:32.890 --> 00:22:35.163
that simply weren't economic to keep up.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:39.770
If your whole life has been
associated with a place,

00:22:39.770 --> 00:22:42.080
it is really hard to admit that that place

00:22:42.080 --> 00:22:43.480
need never have existed

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:45.850
or that it won't exist in the future.

00:22:45.850 --> 00:22:48.010
Even if that place is widely famed

00:22:48.010 --> 00:22:49.740
for atrocity and stupidity,

00:22:49.740 --> 00:22:52.460
even if it's notoriously unpleasant,

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:53.760
it is really hard to admit

00:22:53.760 --> 00:22:56.393
that it ought not to
have been built at all.

00:22:57.860 --> 00:23:01.370
But if Genia who was
the daughter of victims

00:23:02.210 --> 00:23:04.610
was unable to understand

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:08.160
the strange economics
situation of her city

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:10.780
and the role it played in history,

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:12.240
then who can?

00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:15.410
And this question brings me
to the next part of this talk

00:23:15.410 --> 00:23:19.690
in which I'd like to ask why the Gulag,

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:22.620
about which historians now know so much,

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:23.900
since my books has been written

00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:26.070
there have been many others,

00:23:26.070 --> 00:23:28.270
there's a fantastic amount of research

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:30.370
that is widely available,

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:34.020
and whose economic impact
we understand so much better

00:23:34.020 --> 00:23:38.020
and whose psychological
impact we understand as well,

00:23:38.020 --> 00:23:42.000
why is it so seldom debated and discussed

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:43.590
by modern Russians?

00:23:43.590 --> 00:23:46.460
And in this I'll talk about
the Gulag in a larger sense,

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:48.440
why is the Gulag rarely discussed?

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:51.050
Why are Stalin's crimes rarely discussed?

00:23:51.050 --> 00:23:55.820
Why is it so disappeared from
the modern Russian debate?

00:23:55.820 --> 00:23:59.170
I mean, one of the things
that always strikes

00:23:59.170 --> 00:24:01.350
contemporary visitors to Russia

00:24:01.350 --> 00:24:04.470
is the lack of monuments
to Stalin's victims.

00:24:04.470 --> 00:24:07.740
So, there are a few scattered memorials

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:10.500
but there's really no national monument

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.070
or place of mourning.

00:24:12.070 --> 00:24:14.890
And actually the absence of monuments

00:24:14.890 --> 00:24:19.303
is an accurate reflection of
the absence of public debate.

00:24:20.520 --> 00:24:23.010
I should say this was not always the case.

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:26.620
So there were wide-ranging
discussion of Soviet repression

00:24:26.620 --> 00:24:27.670
in the 1980s.

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:29.640
This is the very end of the Soviet period

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.890
during the reign of Mikhail Gorbachev.

00:24:31.890 --> 00:24:34.270
And actually these discussions played

00:24:34.270 --> 00:24:36.290
an important maybe key,

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:39.450
maybe absolutely central role

00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:41.870
in delegitimizing the Soviet regime.

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:45.250
So once, as I mentioned at
the beginning of this lecture,

00:24:45.250 --> 00:24:46.825
all these subjects were kept,

00:24:46.825 --> 00:24:48.560
they were heavily secret,

00:24:48.560 --> 00:24:51.380
they were not talked about
while the Soviet Union existed,

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:53.800
and in this outpour of glasnost,

00:24:53.800 --> 00:24:56.260
this moment when suddenly
all kinds of things

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:58.100
were possible to talk about in the 1980s,

00:24:58.100 --> 00:24:59.620
one of the first things that happened is

00:24:59.620 --> 00:25:03.240
people began to talk about the camp system

00:25:03.240 --> 00:25:04.960
and the crimes of the camp system,

00:25:04.960 --> 00:25:07.070
the crimes of the Stalin era.

00:25:07.070 --> 00:25:09.960
And people then began to ask

00:25:09.960 --> 00:25:11.920
if this system that we're still part of

00:25:11.920 --> 00:25:14.327
created all these horrors,

00:25:14.327 --> 00:25:16.323
why should we support it anymore?

00:25:17.178 --> 00:25:19.803
And as I said these were
really important discussions

00:25:19.803 --> 00:25:21.920
at the time,

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:24.570
but this debate, the
bitter debate at the time

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.240
about justice for the victims

00:25:26.240 --> 00:25:28.140
is now completely over

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:32.480
and it really left no political
institutions in its wake.

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.570
And so although there
was much talk about it

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:36.210
at the end of the 1980s,

00:25:36.210 --> 00:25:38.340
the Russian government never did examine

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:42.140
or try the perpetrators
of torture or mass murder,

00:25:42.140 --> 00:25:45.510
even those who were still
alive or identifiable.

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:47.260
There were no truth commissions,

00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:49.260
there was no natural debate

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:51.890
of the kind implemented in South Africa

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:54.720
which might have allowed
victims to tell their stories

00:25:54.720 --> 00:25:57.613
in an official public
sphere, in a public venue.

00:25:59.614 --> 00:26:02.623
There was certainly no
compensation for victims.

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:09.020
At some level the reasons for
this are not hard to fathom.

00:26:09.020 --> 00:26:13.810
Even by 1990-91, the Stalinist
era had been long passed.

00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:15.590
The last Stalinist camps closed

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:19.140
after Stalin died in the 1950s.

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:21.810
There had been a smaller camp system

00:26:21.810 --> 00:26:23.480
just for political prisoners

00:26:23.480 --> 00:26:25.200
that lasted right up to
the end of the Soviet Union

00:26:25.200 --> 00:26:27.060
and has now been revived under Putin.

00:26:27.060 --> 00:26:28.970
But the era of mass incarceration

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:31.030
had been over for a long time

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:35.060
and a great deal had
happened since it had ended.

00:26:35.060 --> 00:26:38.270
Post-Soviet Russia was not the
same as post-Soviet Germany

00:26:38.270 --> 00:26:41.010
where memories of the worst atrocities

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:44.550
were still very much
present in people's minds.

00:26:44.550 --> 00:26:48.100
The memories of the camps
is also confuse in Russia

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:50.900
by the presence of so
many other atrocities,

00:26:50.900 --> 00:26:51.910
the other things to remember.

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:55.993
You know, the war, the
famines, collectivization,

00:26:56.908 --> 00:26:58.140
the mass murder.

00:26:58.140 --> 00:27:00.190
Why should camp survivors get

00:27:00.190 --> 00:27:02.583
some kind of privileged
status or treatment?

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:08.860
I think in a way the problem
is made even more confusing

00:27:09.970 --> 00:27:13.490
by the link that was made
in some people's minds

00:27:13.490 --> 00:27:16.020
between that discussion of the past

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:18.850
that took place in the
1980s, which I've mentioned,

00:27:18.850 --> 00:27:22.380
and the collapse of the
economy in the 1990s.

00:27:22.380 --> 00:27:23.470
So people would say,

00:27:23.470 --> 00:27:25.797
people used to say this to me in Russia,

00:27:25.797 --> 00:27:29.397
"What was the point of
talking about all of that?

00:27:29.397 --> 00:27:31.167
"It didn't get us anywhere."

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:34.930
There's also a kind of question of pride.

00:27:34.930 --> 00:27:38.500
Like Genia, many people experienced

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:39.940
the collapse of the Soviet Union

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:41.963
as a kind of personal blow.

00:27:41.963 --> 00:27:43.812
It altered their identity,

00:27:43.812 --> 00:27:46.340
their world made no
sense after it was gone.

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.443
Who were they, what was their nationality?

00:27:49.550 --> 00:27:53.500
But also perhaps the old system
was bad they might have felt

00:27:53.500 --> 00:27:56.190
but at least we were
powerful and important,

00:27:56.190 --> 00:27:58.220
and now that we are not powerful

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:00.673
we really don't wanna
hear that it was bad.

00:28:01.900 --> 00:28:04.630
Still, I think that the
most important explanation

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:06.640
for the lack of debate,

00:28:06.640 --> 00:28:08.890
and here I'm gonna be interested,

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.150
I would be interested to hear

00:28:10.150 --> 00:28:13.120
how you're gonna compare this
to the American experience,

00:28:13.120 --> 00:28:15.710
the most important explanation
for the lack of debate

00:28:15.710 --> 00:28:19.320
is not really the fears and
anxieties of ordinary Russians

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:21.170
but it was really to do with the nature

00:28:21.170 --> 00:28:23.060
of the country's new ruling class

00:28:23.060 --> 00:28:25.560
who remain the current ruling class.

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:28.200
So, Vladimir Putin who's
the president of Russia

00:28:28.200 --> 00:28:30.390
is a former KGB officer

00:28:30.390 --> 00:28:33.140
who has described himself
using the word Chekist,

00:28:33.140 --> 00:28:36.793
which is a word that Lenin
used for the political police.

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:41.870
Famously, Putin has also described

00:28:41.870 --> 00:28:43.420
the breakup of the Soviet Union

00:28:43.420 --> 00:28:47.150
as the greatest geopolitical catastrophe

00:28:47.150 --> 00:28:48.700
of the 20th century,

00:28:48.700 --> 00:28:53.450
presumably meaning greater
than World Wars I or II.

00:28:53.450 --> 00:28:56.840
So under his watch Soviet
flags have returned

00:28:56.840 --> 00:28:59.100
to anniversary parades,

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:01.380
the Soviet national
anthem has been revived,

00:29:01.380 --> 00:29:05.860
Soviet symbolism of all
kinds has been brought back.

00:29:05.860 --> 00:29:09.240
And the reason for this is quite clear.

00:29:09.240 --> 00:29:12.350
Putin is trying to create
an alternate version

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:13.840
of post-Soviet history,

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:17.970
one which supports his
claims to rule the country.

00:29:17.970 --> 00:29:20.320
So, by his version of the past,

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.610
1991, which is the year
the Soviet Union broke up,

00:29:23.610 --> 00:29:26.070
was not a moment of liberation.

00:29:26.070 --> 00:29:28.730
It was the beginning of economic collapse.

00:29:28.730 --> 00:29:31.800
The hardships and
depravations of the 1990s

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:35.120
were not the result of
decades of communist neglect

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:36.390
and widespread theft

00:29:36.390 --> 00:29:39.072
but of Western-style democracy.

00:29:39.072 --> 00:29:41.700
Communism was stable and safe,

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:44.240
post-communism was a disaster.

00:29:44.240 --> 00:29:46.050
The Soviet Union was great.

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:48.300
Russia, at least until Putin took over,

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:50.150
was a failure.

00:29:50.150 --> 00:29:53.380
The more people believe
that version of history,

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:55.540
the less likely they are to want a system

00:29:55.540 --> 00:29:57.870
that is more genuinely democratic

00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:02.180
and which offers people real
opportunities to advance

00:30:02.180 --> 00:30:05.873
which isn't a top-down
controlled kleptocracy.

00:30:06.880 --> 00:30:08.680
The more nostalgia,

00:30:08.680 --> 00:30:13.250
but on the contrary, the more
nostalgia for the Soviet era,

00:30:13.250 --> 00:30:17.660
the more secure Putin and
his kind of ex-KGB clique

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:18.993
is likely to be.

00:30:20.320 --> 00:30:23.410
So, in other words, the
failure to discuss the past

00:30:23.410 --> 00:30:25.780
or dwell on the past or repent the past

00:30:25.780 --> 00:30:27.870
or focus on the past

00:30:27.870 --> 00:30:31.360
is a part of this redirecting of history

00:30:31.360 --> 00:30:34.063
towards the political needs
of the present rulers.

00:30:34.950 --> 00:30:37.500
But it has also had consequences

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:40.180
for the formation of
Russia's civil society

00:30:40.180 --> 00:30:42.880
and for the development of rule of law.

00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:44.540
It helps explain, for example,

00:30:44.540 --> 00:30:48.120
why Russians are not as
bothered as they should be

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:49.770
by certain kinds of censorship

00:30:49.770 --> 00:30:54.770
or by the new tactic of naming
any independent journalist

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:56.700
as enemies of the state.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:59.550
They're now called foreign
agents but it's the same idea.

00:31:01.636 --> 00:31:03.350
Why when the FSB,

00:31:03.350 --> 00:31:05.645
which is the modern
incarnation of the KGB,

00:31:05.645 --> 00:31:08.080
when they are given free rein

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:11.463
to open mail or tap telephones
or enter private residences

00:31:11.463 --> 00:31:12.590
without a court order

00:31:12.590 --> 00:31:14.713
why there's not more objection to this.

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:18.940
The sense that there needed to be a break

00:31:18.940 --> 00:31:19.920
with the old system,

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:23.970
that the lack of rule of law
demands a profound change

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:25.950
never really happened.

00:31:25.950 --> 00:31:29.350
I think there's a kind of
deeper significance too.

00:31:29.350 --> 00:31:32.060
To put it bluntly,

00:31:32.060 --> 00:31:35.693
if the scoundrels of the old
regime all went unpunished,

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:40.880
there was no justice in the new system,

00:31:40.880 --> 00:31:44.667
and so good didn't triumph after 1991.

00:31:44.667 --> 00:31:46.860
And this may sound a
little bit apocalyptic

00:31:46.860 --> 00:31:49.160
but it's not politically irrelevant.

00:31:49.160 --> 00:31:53.810
So, after 1991, when the
Soviet Union broke up,

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:56.330
the secret policemen, the
leaders of the old system,

00:31:56.330 --> 00:31:59.380
they kept their apartments,
their country houses,

00:31:59.380 --> 00:32:01.030
their large pensions,

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:03.280
and their victims, the former prisoners,

00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:05.540
remained poor and marginal.

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:07.660
And so to most Russians it now seems

00:32:07.660 --> 00:32:10.140
as if the more you
collaborated in the past,

00:32:10.140 --> 00:32:11.523
the wiser you were.

00:32:13.635 --> 00:32:16.890
If you were a winner
then, you're a winner now.

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:20.310
And by analogy, the more you cheat and lie

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:22.070
and stay within the system in the present,

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:24.375
the wiser you are now too.

00:32:24.375 --> 00:32:27.694
That there's no benefit from justice.

00:32:27.694 --> 00:32:29.574
You don't gain anything

00:32:29.574 --> 00:32:33.720
from seeking redress for past crimes.

00:32:33.720 --> 00:32:35.360
Personally, what bothers me the most

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:39.060
about Russia's lack of interest
in this part of its past

00:32:39.060 --> 00:32:42.990
is the way it's deprived young
people some of their heroes.

00:32:42.990 --> 00:32:45.910
The names of those who
secretly opposed Stalin

00:32:45.910 --> 00:32:47.840
ought to be as widely known in Russia

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:49.460
as they are in Germany, for example,

00:32:49.460 --> 00:32:53.340
as the names of the participants
in the plot to kill Hitler.

00:32:53.340 --> 00:32:56.850
The incredibly rich body of
Russian survivors literature

00:32:56.850 --> 00:32:58.280
which I used to write book,

00:32:58.280 --> 00:33:01.580
these are tales of people
whose humanity triumphed

00:33:01.580 --> 00:33:03.240
over horrifying conditions,

00:33:03.240 --> 00:33:07.430
and there's amazing stories
of friendship and bravery.

00:33:07.430 --> 00:33:09.240
These should be better
read and better known

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:10.763
and more frequently quoted.

00:33:11.600 --> 00:33:13.870
If school children knew these heroes

00:33:13.870 --> 00:33:14.820
and their stories better

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.310
they would find something to be proud of

00:33:17.310 --> 00:33:18.880
in Russia's Soviet past

00:33:18.880 --> 00:33:21.693
aside from imperial and military triumphs.

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:24.410
This, after all, is the
country that invented

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:26.190
the modern human rights movement,

00:33:26.190 --> 00:33:28.790
and yet I don't think most
Russians think of that

00:33:28.790 --> 00:33:30.210
or remember it.

00:33:30.210 --> 00:33:35.210
Instead, that part of Russia's
past remains very closed,

00:33:36.410 --> 00:33:40.110
almost like a kind of
unopened Pandora's box.

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:43.163
It lies in wait for the next generation.

00:33:44.970 --> 00:33:48.750
But do we, in the US or in Europe,

00:33:48.750 --> 00:33:52.050
do we remember the Soviet past

00:33:52.050 --> 00:33:54.010
which is a part of Soviet history,

00:33:54.010 --> 00:33:55.940
it's a part of world history,

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:57.963
do we remember it any better?

00:33:58.860 --> 00:34:01.070
One of the reasons I wrote my Gulag book,

00:34:01.070 --> 00:34:04.730
this was one of my
inspirations in the late 1990s,

00:34:04.730 --> 00:34:08.090
was because I really
encountered this subject myself

00:34:08.090 --> 00:34:09.920
only while living in Eastern Europe.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:11.730
I remember the first person I met

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:13.790
who had been a Gulag prisoner

00:34:13.790 --> 00:34:15.360
was in Vilnius, in Lithuania.

00:34:15.360 --> 00:34:17.520
It was a man who explained to me

00:34:17.520 --> 00:34:21.670
how you could pack lots of
people into the back of a truck

00:34:21.670 --> 00:34:24.010
because he remembered how it had been done

00:34:25.770 --> 00:34:29.690
in the 1940s when he was
deported along with his family.

00:34:29.690 --> 00:34:32.560
But this subject wasn't
taught in my school,

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:35.460
even though I studied
Russian history at Yale.

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:37.580
It wasn't subject of great interest,

00:34:37.580 --> 00:34:41.170
it wasn't a major part of any curriculum,

00:34:41.170 --> 00:34:43.700
we didn't have a course like the one

00:34:43.700 --> 00:34:47.853
Professors Snyder and
Stanley are teaching today.

00:34:49.010 --> 00:34:50.640
The Gulag when I was growing up

00:34:50.640 --> 00:34:53.050
was not really part of
popular culture either

00:34:53.050 --> 00:34:55.642
the way that Nazi camps were.

00:34:55.642 --> 00:34:57.320
You have an image in your mind

00:34:57.320 --> 00:34:59.160
of what a Nazi camp looks like

00:34:59.160 --> 00:35:01.540
but not of a Gulag camp.

00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:03.660
But it wasn't really part
of standard histories

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:04.550
of the 20th century,

00:35:04.550 --> 00:35:07.433
it just wasn't a subject
that you encountered.

00:35:08.992 --> 00:35:10.930
And I spent a lot of time

00:35:10.930 --> 00:35:13.430
thinking about why that was as well,

00:35:13.430 --> 00:35:17.220
and I felt once again it
was possible to explain

00:35:17.220 --> 00:35:19.140
this absence of popular feeling

00:35:19.140 --> 00:35:21.400
about the tragedy of European communism.

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:24.360
Also there were some logical explanations.

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:27.440
Again, the passage of time was part of it.

00:35:27.440 --> 00:35:30.310
Communist regimes also
grew less reprehensible

00:35:30.310 --> 00:35:31.820
as time went on.

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:34.460
Nobody was very afraid
of General Jaruzelski

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:37.540
who was the last communist
dictator of Poland.

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:41.320
Nobody was frightened of
the final Soviet leaders,

00:35:41.320 --> 00:35:42.850
even Brezhnev and Andropov,

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:44.790
as much as they were frightened of Stalin,

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:46.910
although both were responsible

00:35:46.910 --> 00:35:48.950
for a great deal of destruction.

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:52.210
But besides, as I've said,
the archives were closed.

00:35:52.210 --> 00:35:54.970
Access to campsites was forbidden.

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:58.400
No television cameras ever
filmed the Soviet camps

00:35:58.400 --> 00:35:59.760
or their victims

00:35:59.760 --> 00:36:01.850
as they had done in Germany

00:36:01.850 --> 00:36:03.650
at the end of the Second World War.

00:36:03.650 --> 00:36:05.970
And no images in turn meant

00:36:05.970 --> 00:36:09.020
that the subject in our
image-driven culture

00:36:09.020 --> 00:36:11.100
didn't really exist either.

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:12.690
To this day it's very difficult

00:36:12.690 --> 00:36:14.460
to make documentaries about the Gulag

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:16.080
because there just aren't photographs,

00:36:16.080 --> 00:36:17.193
there aren't pictures.

00:36:18.280 --> 00:36:23.180
But ideology also shaped the
way in which we understood

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:25.490
Soviet and East European history as well

00:36:25.490 --> 00:36:27.900
for as long as that system existed.

00:36:27.900 --> 00:36:31.180
And this is an interesting
point that again you'll find

00:36:31.180 --> 00:36:35.360
might have further echoes in
the course that you're doing.

00:36:35.360 --> 00:36:37.920
In Europe and the US
you can actually trace

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:40.760
the ups and downs of
knowledge of the Soviet Union.

00:36:40.760 --> 00:36:44.900
So, at different times people
knew more or less about it,

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:47.700
at different times the
story figured more or less

00:36:47.700 --> 00:36:49.870
in the popular press.

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:52.640
In other words, the politics of the West,

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:53.950
so the politics of Europe,

00:36:53.950 --> 00:36:55.090
the politics of the United States,

00:36:55.090 --> 00:36:59.630
actually affected the way we
understood Soviet politics.

00:36:59.630 --> 00:37:01.100
So, for example,

00:37:01.100 --> 00:37:05.740
in the 1920s a great deal was
known in Western capitals,

00:37:05.740 --> 00:37:09.600
in Berlin, in London, in Washington,

00:37:09.600 --> 00:37:12.630
about the bloodiness
of Lenin's revolution.

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:15.340
And that was because the first victims,

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:17.100
as I mentioned already,

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:19.630
of the revolution were socialists.

00:37:19.630 --> 00:37:21.460
And Western socialists.

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:25.170
So, again, in all the important capitals

00:37:25.170 --> 00:37:27.760
knew that their brethren, their comrades,

00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:29.190
had been jailed by the Bolsheviks,

00:37:29.190 --> 00:37:33.100
and the first people to really
protest loudly and strongly

00:37:33.100 --> 00:37:37.080
against the crimes being
committed by the revolution

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:39.363
were on the left.

00:37:40.920 --> 00:37:42.550
However, in the 1930s,

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:45.370
as Americans and West Europeans
became more interested

00:37:45.370 --> 00:37:48.780
in learning how socialism
could be applied at home,

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:50.850
this was a time of
economic crisis in the West

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:53.790
and people were looking
for other solutions,

00:37:53.790 --> 00:37:54.950
the tone changes

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:59.504
and then American and European
writers go off to the USSR

00:37:59.504 --> 00:38:02.356
and they try to use to learn lessons

00:38:02.356 --> 00:38:04.960
that could be brought back.

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:07.260
Famously, some of what later became

00:38:08.260 --> 00:38:10.060
Franklin Roosevelt's kitchen cabinet

00:38:10.060 --> 00:38:11.030
went to the Soviet Union

00:38:11.030 --> 00:38:13.350
to see if there was
something they could learn.

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:14.910
The New York Times at that time

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:17.660
employed a correspondent, Walter Duranty,

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:20.040
who lauded the five-year plan

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:22.040
and argued against a lot of evidence

00:38:22.040 --> 00:38:24.120
that it was a massive success.

00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:27.090
He also argued that
collectivization was not a disaster

00:38:27.090 --> 00:38:29.980
and he covered up the
stories of the famine,

00:38:29.980 --> 00:38:30.840
specially in Ukraine,

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:32.460
even though he knew they were true,

00:38:32.460 --> 00:38:34.600
and he won a Pulitzer Prize for doing so

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:36.740
because this is what
people wanted to hear.

00:38:36.740 --> 00:38:39.630
There was a moment when people
didn't wanna hear anymore

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:41.330
about why the revolution was bad,

00:38:41.330 --> 00:38:43.190
they wanted to hear what was succeeding,

00:38:43.190 --> 00:38:45.770
or what they thought was succeeding.

00:38:45.770 --> 00:38:49.980
Throughout the 1930s and 40s, a part of,

00:38:49.980 --> 00:38:51.970
I would say it's not
even the Western left,

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:55.120
I would say mainstream Western politicians

00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:58.340
struggled to explain and
sometimes excuse the camps,

00:38:58.340 --> 00:39:01.140
because the stories would filter through,

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:02.570
and the terror that created them

00:39:02.570 --> 00:39:04.140
precisely because they wanted to try

00:39:04.140 --> 00:39:06.460
some aspects of the
Soviet experiment at home.

00:39:06.460 --> 00:39:09.480
So our politics shaped our understanding

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:11.370
of the Soviet Union.

00:39:11.370 --> 00:39:13.790
And this became even more complicated

00:39:13.790 --> 00:39:16.090
during the Second World War

00:39:16.090 --> 00:39:18.560
when Stalin was our ally.

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:22.570
So in 1944 the American
Vice President Henry Wallace

00:39:22.570 --> 00:39:23.970
actually went to Kolyma,

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:26.030
which is one of the most notorious camps,

00:39:26.030 --> 00:39:28.790
during a trip across the USSR,

00:39:28.790 --> 00:39:31.410
which he said reminded
him of the Wild West.

00:39:31.410 --> 00:39:33.037
There's a famous quote where he says,

00:39:33.037 --> 00:39:37.100
"Soviet Asia reminds me
so much of my homeland."

00:39:37.100 --> 00:39:40.310
According to a report
that the boss of Kolyma

00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:41.680
later wrote for Beria

00:39:41.680 --> 00:39:44.530
who was then the head of
the Soviet Secret Services,

00:39:44.530 --> 00:39:46.340
Wallace did ask to see prisoners,

00:39:46.340 --> 00:39:47.540
so he knew where he was,

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:51.372
he knew he was seeing a
site of mass incarceration,

00:39:51.372 --> 00:39:54.070
but he was kept away from them.

00:39:54.070 --> 00:39:58.070
He was not alone in not wanting
to know this piece of truth,

00:39:58.070 --> 00:40:01.500
this aspect of the Soviet system.

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:04.330
Obviously at that moment
Roosevelt and Churchill

00:40:04.330 --> 00:40:06.173
were meeting regularly with Stalin.

00:40:07.030 --> 00:40:08.970
They had their photographs taken with him,

00:40:08.970 --> 00:40:12.310
they saw him as an ally in
their war against Hitler,

00:40:12.310 --> 00:40:13.960
and so they were simply not interested

00:40:13.960 --> 00:40:15.963
in bringing up this subject.

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:19.660
All of that is part of what contributed

00:40:19.660 --> 00:40:23.480
to our firm conviction
that the Second World War

00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:25.580
was a holy just war.

00:40:25.580 --> 00:40:29.470
And even today very few people
want that conviction shaken.

00:40:29.470 --> 00:40:30.740
So we remember D-Day,

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:34.440
we remember the liberation of
the Nazi concentration camps,

00:40:34.440 --> 00:40:38.150
we remember children
welcoming American GIs

00:40:38.150 --> 00:40:39.970
with cheers on the streets,

00:40:39.970 --> 00:40:43.840
and we don't remember that
the camps of Stalin, our ally,

00:40:43.840 --> 00:40:45.820
expanded into Eastern Europe,

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:48.220
this is the topic of my
book that Tim mentioned,

00:40:49.797 --> 00:40:51.870
on the expansion of the Soviet system

00:40:51.870 --> 00:40:55.910
into Poland and
Czechoslovakia and Hungary.

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:59.200
We don't remember that
those camps expanded

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:02.990
just as the camps of Hitler,
our enemy, were liberated.

00:41:02.990 --> 00:41:07.620
So nobody wants to think that
we defeated one mass murderer

00:41:07.620 --> 00:41:08.913
with the help of another.

00:41:10.530 --> 00:41:12.300
During the Cold War, it's true,

00:41:12.300 --> 00:41:15.040
our awareness of Soviet
atrocities went up again.

00:41:15.040 --> 00:41:17.420
Again, this reflected American politics

00:41:17.420 --> 00:41:18.800
and American political needs

00:41:18.800 --> 00:41:21.700
rather than anything that
was happening in the USSR.

00:41:21.700 --> 00:41:25.230
In the 1960s they receded again.

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:27.840
Even in the 1980s when I was at Yale

00:41:27.840 --> 00:41:29.470
there were still American academics

00:41:29.470 --> 00:41:31.450
who would describe the advantages

00:41:31.450 --> 00:41:34.340
of East German healthcare
or Polish peace initiatives.

00:41:34.340 --> 00:41:37.951
They would try to push the
values of these systems.

00:41:37.951 --> 00:41:40.700
And at the time when
I was an undergraduate

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:42.200
in the academic world

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:44.800
Soviet historians who
wrote about the camps

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:48.650
were kind of generally
divided into two groups.

00:41:48.650 --> 00:41:50.740
Those who wrote about
the camps as criminal

00:41:50.740 --> 00:41:53.230
and they were called kind of right wing,

00:41:53.230 --> 00:41:54.680
and those who downplayed them

00:41:54.680 --> 00:41:56.650
if not because they
were actually pro-Soviet

00:41:56.650 --> 00:41:59.080
but because they were
opposed to America's role

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:00.530
in the Cold War

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:02.730
or maybe Ronald Reagan.

00:42:02.730 --> 00:42:03.920
In other words,

00:42:03.920 --> 00:42:07.450
one's attitude towards the
Soviet Union and its camps

00:42:07.450 --> 00:42:10.020
was very often shaped
by one's own politics,

00:42:10.020 --> 00:42:11.780
and I'm saying this neutrally.

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:13.950
I'm not saying that one
side was bad or good.

00:42:13.950 --> 00:42:16.600
That your view of the USSR

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:19.720
was shaped by your view of
America and the Cold War.

00:42:19.720 --> 00:42:23.110
And really right up until
the very end of the Cold War

00:42:23.110 --> 00:42:25.790
our views of the Soviet Union
and its repressive system

00:42:25.790 --> 00:42:28.920
always had more to do
with Americans politics

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:31.120
and American ideological struggles

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:33.030
than they did with the
Soviet Union itself.

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:36.190
I mean, it's a lesson that we see things

00:42:36.190 --> 00:42:39.090
through the lens of our own time

00:42:39.090 --> 00:42:42.120
and we found it difficult
to talk about this subject

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:43.260
in a neutral way

00:42:44.100 --> 00:42:46.790
while the Soviet Union still existed.

00:42:46.790 --> 00:42:47.910
I should add that

00:42:49.010 --> 00:42:51.720
there are people who maintain
that this is still true.

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:53.400
I actually don't think so.

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:55.251
I think it's now possible,

00:42:55.251 --> 00:42:59.030
as Tim Snyder does, as many others do,

00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:01.536
to write about Soviet history in a way

00:43:01.536 --> 00:43:04.210
that doesn't reflect your views

00:43:04.210 --> 00:43:06.453
about America or the Cold War or Reagan.

00:43:07.550 --> 00:43:09.007
Because the Soviet Union
doesn't exist anymore

00:43:09.007 --> 00:43:10.880
and because it's a part of history

00:43:10.880 --> 00:43:12.710
you can write about it in a neutral way,

00:43:12.710 --> 00:43:14.170
and one of the reasons there's so much

00:43:14.170 --> 00:43:16.470
good Soviet scholarship in the last years

00:43:16.470 --> 00:43:20.220
is because the ideology is
gone, the struggle is gone,

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:25.220
and now we can begin to see
the system for what it was.

00:43:25.410 --> 00:43:26.243
So, as I said,

00:43:26.243 --> 00:43:28.880
since the collapse of
the Soviet Union in 1991,

00:43:28.880 --> 00:43:30.620
much has changed.

00:43:30.620 --> 00:43:33.870
World War II really now
definitively belongs

00:43:33.870 --> 00:43:36.020
to a previous generation.

00:43:36.020 --> 00:43:38.350
The Cold War is over.

00:43:38.350 --> 00:43:40.840
The alliances and the
international fault lines

00:43:40.840 --> 00:43:41.690
that it produced

00:43:41.690 --> 00:43:43.790
have shifted for good.

00:43:43.790 --> 00:43:45.530
The Western left and the Western right,

00:43:45.530 --> 00:43:47.260
if you can even use those terms anymore,

00:43:47.260 --> 00:43:49.510
they now compete over different issues.

00:43:49.510 --> 00:43:51.990
And so finally it's become possible

00:43:51.990 --> 00:43:55.220
to stop looking at the
history of the Soviet Union

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:58.363
through the narrow lens
of Western politics.

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:02.170
And I'll finish by saying
that it's not only possible,

00:44:02.170 --> 00:44:03.955
I believe it's necessary,

00:44:03.955 --> 00:44:07.760
I'm really glad that there's
another generation of students

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:10.030
who will investigate this story,

00:44:10.030 --> 00:44:12.010
who will interest themselves in it.

00:44:12.010 --> 00:44:15.110
Because after all, in an important sense,

00:44:15.110 --> 00:44:17.943
the Gulag is part of our history too.

00:44:18.950 --> 00:44:21.590
Why did we fight the Cold War after all?

00:44:21.590 --> 00:44:24.150
Was it because some crazy
right wing politicians

00:44:24.150 --> 00:44:28.170
in cahoots with the military
industrial complex and the CIA

00:44:28.170 --> 00:44:29.700
because they invented the whole thing

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.210
and they forced two
generations of Americans

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:33.870
to go along with it?

00:44:33.870 --> 00:44:38.050
Or was there something
more important happening?

00:44:38.050 --> 00:44:39.240
I'm not sure we still remember

00:44:39.240 --> 00:44:42.280
what it was that mobilized
us, what kept us together,

00:44:42.280 --> 00:44:45.716
what held the civilization of
the West together for so long

00:44:45.716 --> 00:44:49.640
and what the useful part of that story was

00:44:49.640 --> 00:44:52.770
and what we achieved by doing that.

00:44:52.770 --> 00:44:54.700
But of course it's also the case

00:44:54.700 --> 00:44:56.350
that the history of the Gulag

00:44:56.350 --> 00:44:58.240
belongs in a much broader sense

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.640
to the history of mankind.

00:45:00.640 --> 00:45:04.760
Every one of the 20th century's
mass tragedies was unique.

00:45:04.760 --> 00:45:08.710
The Gulag, the Holocaust,
the Armenian massacre,

00:45:08.710 --> 00:45:10.410
the Nanjing massacre,

00:45:10.410 --> 00:45:13.070
the Cultural Revolution,
the Cambodian Revolution,

00:45:13.070 --> 00:45:14.513
the Bosnian Wars.

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:18.070
Every one of these events had different

00:45:18.070 --> 00:45:21.080
historical and philosophical origins.

00:45:21.080 --> 00:45:24.870
They all arose in circumstances
that will never be repeated.

00:45:24.870 --> 00:45:28.680
But it is our ability
to debase and destroy

00:45:28.680 --> 00:45:31.830
and once again dehumanize our fellow men,

00:45:31.830 --> 00:45:35.280
this is something that
can and will be repeated

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:36.480
again and again.

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:39.700
It's our ability to
transform our neighbors

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:42.580
or our fellow citizens into enemies.

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:45.430
Our reinvention of our victims

00:45:45.430 --> 00:45:47.900
as lower or lesser or evil beings

00:45:47.900 --> 00:45:51.590
worthy only of incarceration or expulsion

00:45:51.590 --> 00:45:54.520
or forced labor or sometimes death.

00:45:54.520 --> 00:45:56.900
These things are repeated again and again.

00:45:56.900 --> 00:45:59.830
And the more that we
are able to understand

00:45:59.830 --> 00:46:03.460
how different societies
have accomplished this,

00:46:03.460 --> 00:46:05.990
how they have transformed their neighbors

00:46:05.990 --> 00:46:07.990
and fellow citizens into objects,

00:46:07.990 --> 00:46:10.730
the more we know of the
specific circumstances

00:46:10.730 --> 00:46:15.428
of each episode of mass murder
or of mass dehumanization,

00:46:15.428 --> 00:46:17.620
the more that we will understand

00:46:18.590 --> 00:46:21.853
the nature of, you know,
human nature itself.

00:46:23.180 --> 00:46:24.910
I wrote my book about the Gulag

00:46:24.910 --> 00:46:27.620
not so that it will not happen again,

00:46:27.620 --> 00:46:29.900
which is how the cliche goes.

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:33.230
Because it will happen again
and it has happened again.

00:46:33.230 --> 00:46:35.650
And I think we need to know why

00:46:35.650 --> 00:46:38.650
and each story, each memoir,

00:46:38.650 --> 00:46:41.260
each document is a piece of the puzzle.

00:46:41.260 --> 00:46:43.010
If we don't have them,

00:46:43.010 --> 00:46:45.110
we will wake up one day and realize

00:46:45.110 --> 00:46:46.860
that we don't know who we are.

00:46:46.860 --> 00:46:48.260
So, thank you very much.

00:46:48.260 --> 00:46:50.820
I look forward to your questions.

00:46:50.820 --> 00:46:54.070
It's been a pleasure speaking to Yale

00:46:54.070 --> 00:46:56.803
and New Haven residents once again.

00:46:59.080 --> 00:47:00.820
- Anne, thank you very much.

00:47:00.820 --> 00:47:03.630
That was wonderful and that was terrific

00:47:03.630 --> 00:47:06.250
and we're going to have
a lively discussion now.

00:47:06.250 --> 00:47:09.000
I realize that in my introduction

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:11.730
I made at least two mistakes.

00:47:11.730 --> 00:47:14.780
Number one was I forgot
to tell our audience

00:47:14.780 --> 00:47:17.020
that they're supposed
to be asking questions.

00:47:17.020 --> 00:47:21.060
So this is a webinar and if you all look

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:25.990
at the bottom of your screen
to the sort of center right

00:47:25.990 --> 00:47:27.740
there's a thing called Q&amp;A

00:47:27.740 --> 00:47:29.810
where I believe if you
type in your question

00:47:29.810 --> 00:47:30.950
I'll be able to see it

00:47:30.950 --> 00:47:32.770
and then I'll be able as moderator

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:36.263
to pass it on to Anne Applebaum.

00:47:37.180 --> 00:47:38.360
Oh my gosh, yeah,

00:47:38.360 --> 00:47:40.590
the raising hand thing
isn't gonna work though.

00:47:40.590 --> 00:47:42.270
So I see that some of you
are raising your hand.

00:47:42.270 --> 00:47:45.190
It would be cool if instead
you would use the Q&amp;A function

00:47:45.190 --> 00:47:46.260
as one of you just did.

00:47:46.260 --> 00:47:48.283
Okay, great, we're off to a good start.

00:47:49.130 --> 00:47:51.723
My second mistake is
that I forgot to mention

00:47:51.723 --> 00:47:55.030
that Anne is a Yale graduate,

00:47:55.030 --> 00:47:56.210
but fortunately she rescued me

00:47:56.210 --> 00:47:58.992
by mentioning it six
times during the talk.

00:47:58.992 --> 00:48:02.060
- [Anne] Twice.
- Oh sorry, okay, twice.

00:48:02.060 --> 00:48:04.120
She rescued me by mentioning
it during the talk.

00:48:04.120 --> 00:48:05.990
That's all the more reason
we're happy to have you

00:48:05.990 --> 00:48:08.390
as it were back,

00:48:08.390 --> 00:48:11.090
and in the hope to have you back again.

00:48:11.090 --> 00:48:15.060
I'm gonna buy a little time
as the questions accumulate,

00:48:15.060 --> 00:48:17.120
and maybe Professor Stanley
will wanna do the same

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:18.110
and join us,

00:48:18.110 --> 00:48:21.850
just by giving you a
couple of broad questions

00:48:21.850 --> 00:48:23.173
to think about,

00:48:24.150 --> 00:48:27.250
and you can just pick if any of these

00:48:27.250 --> 00:48:28.680
strike you as interesting.

00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:30.080
They have to do with themes

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:34.230
that have already begun
to emerge in the class

00:48:34.230 --> 00:48:36.433
and which you've put a name on too.

00:48:38.364 --> 00:48:42.130
I was in Kiev this weekend

00:48:42.130 --> 00:48:44.160
and I was in Kiev to talk about

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:47.060
this strange sort of open letter

00:48:47.060 --> 00:48:51.680
that Vladimir Putin wrote
about Ukraine and its history,

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:54.100
and this may seem very far apart

00:48:54.100 --> 00:48:56.450
from what we're working on in this class

00:48:56.450 --> 00:48:58.810
and it may be the influence
of this class on me

00:48:58.810 --> 00:49:00.800
that I read it this way.

00:49:00.800 --> 00:49:03.010
So Mr. Putin wrote this long letter,

00:49:03.010 --> 00:49:04.900
as some of you will know,

00:49:04.900 --> 00:49:07.090
basically making a historical case

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:10.020
that the Ukrainian nation doesn't exist

00:49:10.020 --> 00:49:12.390
and concluding that as a result

00:49:12.390 --> 00:49:13.720
it's up to Russia to decide

00:49:13.720 --> 00:49:16.450
whether Ukraine is sovereign or not.

00:49:16.450 --> 00:49:17.810
And as a I was reading this,

00:49:17.810 --> 00:49:19.410
I mean, I read this and I was asked

00:49:19.410 --> 00:49:20.500
to make a public critique of it

00:49:20.500 --> 00:49:23.220
and that's why I was in Kiev
and I said a number of things,

00:49:23.220 --> 00:49:25.470
but one thing which is on
my mind which I didn't say

00:49:25.470 --> 00:49:28.590
was that when I read this
particular form of history,

00:49:28.590 --> 00:49:30.030
that's the right word,

00:49:30.030 --> 00:49:33.788
I was struck by how much it
reminded me of white supremacy.

00:49:33.788 --> 00:49:37.230
That what Mr. Putin was
doing was basically saying,

00:49:37.230 --> 00:49:40.450
he was telling other people
what their history was

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:44.977
and telling them that they
didn't really have a voice in it

00:49:44.977 --> 00:49:48.020
and that he had the final
word in what had happened.

00:49:48.020 --> 00:49:53.020
And in a very broad way
that's a way in which I think

00:49:53.230 --> 00:49:54.710
just beginning to teach this class

00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:56.340
has helped me to make connections,

00:49:56.340 --> 00:49:58.320
'cause I wouldn't have thought of that

00:49:58.320 --> 00:49:59.730
if it hadn't been for this class.

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:00.640
But if you read the letter,

00:50:00.640 --> 00:50:01.753
and I'm gonna recommend to the students

00:50:01.753 --> 00:50:02.910
that you do read this letter

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:05.550
because it's a striking example

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:09.720
of a history that's so one-sided

00:50:09.720 --> 00:50:11.837
where one person doesn't
see any problem in saying,

00:50:11.837 --> 00:50:15.970
"You were happy, everything
was fine, nothing was wrong."

00:50:15.970 --> 00:50:18.440
But this leads me to a
theme which is in your book

00:50:18.440 --> 00:50:19.530
and in your other work

00:50:19.530 --> 00:50:21.750
but didn't come out so much in the lecture

00:50:21.750 --> 00:50:23.770
so maybe I'll just ask you about it,

00:50:23.770 --> 00:50:28.180
and that's the theme of
ethnicity and nationality.

00:50:28.180 --> 00:50:30.620
It's very striking, as you know,

00:50:30.620 --> 00:50:31.800
that at particular moments,

00:50:31.800 --> 00:50:33.384
specially around collectivization

00:50:33.384 --> 00:50:35.750
and then at the end of
the Second World War,

00:50:35.750 --> 00:50:36.620
and also during the period

00:50:36.620 --> 00:50:38.350
of political prisoners in the 1970s,

00:50:38.350 --> 00:50:42.230
Ukrainians are vastly
overrepresented in the camps.

00:50:42.230 --> 00:50:44.070
And so I'm wondering if you would,

00:50:44.070 --> 00:50:46.911
and then you mentioned your friend Genia

00:50:46.911 --> 00:50:49.761
who's Polish and whose parents
probably called her Genka,

00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:52.970
and the Poles were also
vastly overrepresented.

00:50:52.970 --> 00:50:54.070
They were a tiny minority

00:50:54.070 --> 00:50:55.440
but they were vastly overrepresented

00:50:55.440 --> 00:50:57.740
in the camps and in the shootings.

00:50:57.740 --> 00:51:00.250
I wonder how at the end of your research

00:51:00.250 --> 00:51:01.833
you think about this.

00:51:01.833 --> 00:51:04.170
If it's an economic,

00:51:04.170 --> 00:51:07.720
if it's on the surface economic

00:51:07.720 --> 00:51:10.650
but then it's also about punishment,

00:51:10.650 --> 00:51:13.630
why do we explain in this system

00:51:13.630 --> 00:51:16.500
which is supposed to be
after all internationalist,

00:51:16.500 --> 00:51:18.550
how do we explain that
certain kinds of people

00:51:18.550 --> 00:51:19.520
end up being punished

00:51:19.520 --> 00:51:22.573
in such obviously greater
numbers than others?

00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:27.130
- Thanks, it's a great question

00:51:27.130 --> 00:51:30.720
because one of the old
cliches about the Soviet Union

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:32.450
used to be that, well,

00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:34.860
the Nazis killed people for racial reasons

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:38.050
and the Soviet Union killed
people for class reasons.

00:51:38.050 --> 00:51:40.670
And one of the other impacts,

00:51:40.670 --> 00:51:41.990
one of the other things you get

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:43.770
from reading Soviet archives,

00:51:43.770 --> 00:51:48.670
which me and many other
people have done since 1990,

00:51:48.670 --> 00:51:52.870
is the extent to which that's
not true becomes clear.

00:51:52.870 --> 00:51:56.045
So what the Soviet Union did was,

00:51:56.045 --> 00:51:58.010
what Stalin's Soviet Union did

00:51:58.010 --> 00:52:01.100
was it identified potential enemies,

00:52:01.100 --> 00:52:03.220
you know, who might be our opposition?

00:52:03.220 --> 00:52:05.363
Who might be in opposition to the state?

00:52:06.420 --> 00:52:10.000
And there were different qualities

00:52:10.000 --> 00:52:11.360
that could make someone an enemy,

00:52:11.360 --> 00:52:12.700
and one of the qualities was

00:52:12.700 --> 00:52:14.500
if you were a former Russian aristocrat

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:16.490
or if you were a former
military officer and so on.

00:52:16.490 --> 00:52:19.930
But another quality that
made you a potential enemy

00:52:19.930 --> 00:52:24.010
was being from a potentially
enemy ethnic group.

00:52:24.010 --> 00:52:29.010
And actually the so-called
Great Terror of 1937 and 1938

00:52:30.210 --> 00:52:33.130
begins with, and again we know
this, this is now documented,

00:52:33.130 --> 00:52:35.020
begins with the purge of Poles.

00:52:35.020 --> 00:52:37.070
And so all over the Soviet Union

00:52:37.070 --> 00:52:40.132
there's not millions and millions

00:52:40.132 --> 00:52:43.980
but there are many Polish citizens

00:52:43.980 --> 00:52:47.030
because of the way the borders
have moved over history,

00:52:47.030 --> 00:52:50.690
because part of what was once Poland

00:52:50.690 --> 00:52:51.910
later became Russia and so on,

00:52:51.910 --> 00:52:54.570
there are a number of
ethnically Polish citizens

00:52:54.570 --> 00:52:58.070
lives in the USSR in the 1930s, even then.

00:52:58.070 --> 00:53:00.833
And there's a purge of them,
they're arrested en mass.

00:53:01.930 --> 00:53:05.160
Anybody with a Polish surname
becomes liable for arrest.

00:53:05.160 --> 00:53:07.376
And then a similar,

00:53:07.376 --> 00:53:12.320
there's a similar effect
that affects Ukrainians

00:53:12.320 --> 00:53:13.433
a little bit later on.

00:53:14.440 --> 00:53:15.780
Ukraine is a little bit different.

00:53:15.780 --> 00:53:19.570
It's bigger, there's
actually Ukrainian, you know,

00:53:19.570 --> 00:53:24.570
a Ukrainian republic
within the Soviet Union.

00:53:24.970 --> 00:53:26.170
But the Ukrainians,

00:53:26.170 --> 00:53:27.790
as you know and as you've written about

00:53:27.790 --> 00:53:29.110
and I've also written about,

00:53:29.110 --> 00:53:31.460
are then targeted in a different way.

00:53:31.460 --> 00:53:35.540
They're deprived of
food in the early 1930s

00:53:35.540 --> 00:53:37.730
and deliberately left to starve

00:53:37.730 --> 00:53:41.500
because that then eliminates
this problem of Ukraine,

00:53:41.500 --> 00:53:43.540
that Stalin perceives Ukraine as a problem

00:53:43.540 --> 00:53:46.830
because the Ukrainians
haven't entirely accepted

00:53:46.830 --> 00:53:47.710
the Soviet ideology.

00:53:47.710 --> 00:53:51.113
So what you see is that
ethnicity becomes one of the,

00:53:52.470 --> 00:53:55.910
and it's not as if the
Poles were arrested in 1937

00:53:55.910 --> 00:53:57.800
or the Ukrainians were murdered in 1933,

00:53:57.800 --> 00:54:00.370
it's not as if anybody has really examined

00:54:00.370 --> 00:54:02.880
whether these people
are pro or anti Soviet

00:54:02.880 --> 00:54:04.620
or what their political views are.

00:54:04.620 --> 00:54:08.270
They're targeted because
nationality and ethnicity

00:54:08.270 --> 00:54:10.663
have become a kind of symbolic marker.

00:54:12.371 --> 00:54:14.540
They're used as, ah, you were this,

00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:16.850
therefore you're probably an enemy.

00:54:16.850 --> 00:54:21.850
Your nationality is a kind
of identifying quality.

00:54:22.300 --> 00:54:25.510
And when we are seeking
to eliminate all enemies

00:54:25.510 --> 00:54:27.947
or we're seeking to cleanse the system

00:54:27.947 --> 00:54:31.460
to make sure that only
our people are inside it,

00:54:31.460 --> 00:54:34.250
one of the markers that's used is that.

00:54:34.250 --> 00:54:35.850
And all through the Soviet Union

00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:38.640
as you know there are different phases,

00:54:38.640 --> 00:54:39.600
there are different moments

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:42.010
when different ethnic groups are targeted.

00:54:42.010 --> 00:54:44.160
And actually at the end of Stalin's life

00:54:44.160 --> 00:54:45.510
he began targeting Jews.

00:54:45.510 --> 00:54:47.910
This is the same as
so-called doctors' plot

00:54:47.910 --> 00:54:51.050
was beginning a widespread investigation

00:54:51.050 --> 00:54:52.167
of Jews in the system

00:54:52.167 --> 00:54:53.490
and many Jews were fired

00:54:53.490 --> 00:54:55.530
and this actually even had an echo

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:57.950
in some of the satellite states,

00:54:57.950 --> 00:54:59.140
in the Czech Republic.

00:54:59.140 --> 00:55:00.400
Actually, it wasn't called
the Czech Republic then,

00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:02.130
it was Czechoslovakia.

00:55:02.130 --> 00:55:04.450
In Czechoslovakia there
was a purge of Jews as well

00:55:04.450 --> 00:55:06.370
that was an echo of the Soviet version.

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:09.190
So, different ethnic
groups could be isolated

00:55:09.190 --> 00:55:12.440
and picked on and eliminated

00:55:12.440 --> 00:55:15.360
as a way of fulfilling this anxiety

00:55:15.360 --> 00:55:17.350
the system had about itself.

00:55:17.350 --> 00:55:19.030
I mean, I really do think

00:55:19.030 --> 00:55:22.530
that Putin is anxious about Ukraine,

00:55:22.530 --> 00:55:25.190
and I don't mean in a kind of
Freudian psychological way.

00:55:25.190 --> 00:55:28.990
I mean he's anxious for a reason.

00:55:28.990 --> 00:55:33.530
Because Ukraine poses a
challenge to Russia today,

00:55:33.530 --> 00:55:37.090
and it's the same kind of
challenge Ukraine posed to Stalin.

00:55:37.090 --> 00:55:42.090
Namely, it's a country that is

00:55:42.280 --> 00:55:45.580
closely related ethnically to Russia,

00:55:45.580 --> 00:55:50.520
yet it doesn't accept the
ruling ideology of Russia.

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:52.010
It wants to be different.

00:55:52.010 --> 00:55:55.310
In modern Ukraine's case
it wants a more democratic,

00:55:55.310 --> 00:55:57.760
less kleptocracic political system.

00:55:57.760 --> 00:55:58.680
In the 1930s,

00:55:58.680 --> 00:56:02.060
Ukraine wanted some of its own
national status and language.

00:56:02.060 --> 00:56:04.970
And the reaction of the totalitarian,

00:56:04.970 --> 00:56:07.350
of the autocrat, of the authoritarian

00:56:07.350 --> 00:56:09.310
is to eliminate, as you say,

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:10.730
either eliminate the history

00:56:10.730 --> 00:56:14.600
or eliminate the reality
of these opposing nations.

00:56:14.600 --> 00:56:18.043
And it's a character trait that you can,

00:56:19.160 --> 00:56:20.610
Putinism is not Stalinism,

00:56:20.610 --> 00:56:21.510
they aren't the same thing at all,

00:56:21.510 --> 00:56:26.190
but there are these echoes
from the past into the present

00:56:27.920 --> 00:56:28.963
in which the,

00:56:30.220 --> 00:56:33.840
anybody who's seen as
outside of our group,

00:56:33.840 --> 00:56:37.320
anybody who offers an
alternative view of the world

00:56:37.320 --> 00:56:40.070
has to be eliminated or denied agency

00:56:40.070 --> 00:56:42.230
or removed of their history

00:56:42.230 --> 00:56:45.020
because they're a threat
to the mainstream.

00:56:45.020 --> 00:56:46.280
So in that sense

00:56:47.428 --> 00:56:50.140
it's a really important theme

00:56:50.140 --> 00:56:51.650
all the way through the Soviet Union,

00:56:51.650 --> 00:56:54.510
not something that I think
was sufficiently understood.

00:56:54.510 --> 00:56:56.990
We believe their language
about internationalism

00:56:56.990 --> 00:56:59.010
and so on and class warfare,

00:56:59.010 --> 00:57:02.120
and while the Soviet Union
existed it was much harder to see

00:57:02.120 --> 00:57:04.203
exactly how racist it was.

00:57:05.920 --> 00:57:07.570
- So, Jason, do you wanna come in?

00:57:07.570 --> 00:57:10.090
We've got a bunch of
questions now, so we're good,

00:57:10.090 --> 00:57:12.130
but if you wanna come in first, please do.

00:57:12.130 --> 00:57:13.580
- Yeah, I'm just gonna say

00:57:13.580 --> 00:57:16.180
that one of the questions
of our seminar is,

00:57:16.180 --> 00:57:19.610
how do prison systems become massive?

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:23.370
What are the elements, the
core elements that result

00:57:23.370 --> 00:57:26.530
in the kind of enormous
prison systems you see

00:57:26.530 --> 00:57:29.857
say in the United States
now, in the Soviet Union,

00:57:29.857 --> 00:57:34.080
and whether ethnicity,
targeting an ethnic group,

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:38.150
is necessary for that mass structure,

00:57:38.150 --> 00:57:39.813
or at least always implicated.

00:57:40.830 --> 00:57:45.460
- So, you certainly need an
element of dehumanization.

00:57:45.460 --> 00:57:46.963
In other words, you have to,

00:57:47.885 --> 00:57:49.780
in the Soviet Union it's not only ethnic.

00:57:49.780 --> 00:57:51.620
It can be ethnic and it was often ethnic

00:57:51.620 --> 00:57:53.740
but it wasn't only ethnic.

00:57:53.740 --> 00:57:58.740
But you need to have identified
some group as less worthy,

00:57:59.090 --> 00:58:00.910
or they aren't real citizens,

00:58:00.910 --> 00:58:02.980
or they aren't the same as the rest of us,

00:58:02.980 --> 00:58:07.700
or they are somehow deserving
of being locked up en mass,

00:58:07.700 --> 00:58:09.570
they aren't part of the same,

00:58:09.570 --> 00:58:10.950
they don't have the same status,

00:58:10.950 --> 00:58:12.890
the same citizenship as us.

00:58:12.890 --> 00:58:17.770
And that can be ethnic or
racial, and has often been,

00:58:17.770 --> 00:58:20.189
but it's not only that.

00:58:20.189 --> 00:58:21.670
So, as I said,

00:58:21.670 --> 00:58:25.030
the moment of mass expansion
in the Soviet Union

00:58:25.030 --> 00:58:27.200
is also this moment of collectivization.

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:28.033
For those who don't know,

00:58:28.033 --> 00:58:29.180
that's the moment when

00:58:30.810 --> 00:58:33.980
peasants were forced to
leave their private farms

00:58:33.980 --> 00:58:36.400
and forced onto these collective farms,

00:58:36.400 --> 00:58:38.460
and many resisted, they
didn't wanna do this,

00:58:38.460 --> 00:58:40.620
they didn't wanna give up
their cow to the collective

00:58:40.620 --> 00:58:41.840
and they were worried they would starve,

00:58:41.840 --> 00:58:43.650
which, actually, they did.

00:58:43.650 --> 00:58:45.510
They were right to worry.

00:58:45.510 --> 00:58:47.710
And so there was a mass
arrest of peasants.

00:58:47.710 --> 00:58:48.760
They were kulaks.

00:58:48.760 --> 00:58:51.330
They were given a special
name, anybody who resisted.

00:58:51.330 --> 00:58:53.020
It was supposed to be rich peasants

00:58:53.020 --> 00:58:55.030
but actually anyone who
resisted collectivization

00:58:55.030 --> 00:58:57.950
was named a kulak and they
were liable to arrest.

00:58:57.950 --> 00:59:02.350
And alongside this campaign to arrest them

00:59:02.350 --> 00:59:04.840
there was a massive propaganda effort

00:59:04.840 --> 00:59:06.470
to dehumanize the kulaks.

00:59:06.470 --> 00:59:08.500
The kulaks are preventing the revolution.

00:59:08.500 --> 00:59:10.543
The kulaks are opposed to the rest of us.

00:59:10.543 --> 00:59:13.310
There'd be caricatures of the evil kulak

00:59:13.310 --> 00:59:16.320
with a long beard and sort of evil eyes

00:59:16.320 --> 00:59:18.240
and scary-looking faces

00:59:18.240 --> 00:59:21.370
that you would find in the Soviet press.

00:59:21.370 --> 00:59:25.777
And they would be associated
with the White Guards

00:59:25.777 --> 00:59:27.060
and the aristocrats

00:59:27.060 --> 00:59:30.996
and whatever, Satan and
the devil, almost anything.

00:59:30.996 --> 00:59:34.180
They were made into enemies.

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:35.920
And that was both,

00:59:35.920 --> 00:59:39.520
and that allowed the
guards to accept the fact

00:59:39.520 --> 00:59:40.480
that they were there

00:59:40.480 --> 00:59:43.360
and it also allowed the
rest of the population

00:59:43.360 --> 00:59:45.360
to accept these mass arrests.

00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:49.170
So obviously, if you're
surrounded by evil people

00:59:49.170 --> 00:59:51.140
who are seeking to overthrow the state

00:59:51.140 --> 00:59:52.700
and ruin your revolution

00:59:52.700 --> 00:59:55.500
and they're the ones who are preventing us

00:59:55.500 --> 01:00:00.220
from achieving full
communism and, I don't know,

01:00:00.220 --> 01:00:03.570
manna and honey and milk
and honey for everybody,

01:00:03.570 --> 01:00:05.070
these are the people standing in the way,

01:00:05.070 --> 01:00:07.070
of course you're willing to remove them.

01:00:07.980 --> 01:00:09.270
And there's an echo of this, I mean,

01:00:09.270 --> 01:00:11.150
I spent a little bit when I
was writing the Gulag book

01:00:11.150 --> 01:00:13.700
and I did a little bit
similar in the Ukraine book,

01:00:14.780 --> 01:00:17.340
I spent a lot of time
looking at how the guards

01:00:17.340 --> 01:00:18.980
talked about the prisoners,

01:00:18.980 --> 01:00:21.120
what's the way they spoke about them,

01:00:21.120 --> 01:00:23.030
and I mentioned this in the speech

01:00:23.030 --> 01:00:24.710
but I'll just emphasize it.

01:00:24.710 --> 01:00:28.733
They are seen as lumps of coal.

01:00:30.070 --> 01:00:31.810
They have no humanity.

01:00:31.810 --> 01:00:34.370
When people are outraged
that they are staving,

01:00:34.370 --> 01:00:36.540
it's not because there are hungry people

01:00:36.540 --> 01:00:38.350
but because how do you expect us

01:00:38.350 --> 01:00:40.940
to fulfill the production quotas
with these starving people?

01:00:40.940 --> 01:00:42.440
They're too weak to work.

01:00:42.440 --> 01:00:45.850
In other words, the system
successfully removes them.

01:00:45.850 --> 01:00:48.420
They aren't citizens, they aren't like us,

01:00:48.420 --> 01:00:50.453
they deserve to be in this prison.

01:00:51.650 --> 01:00:56.530
And that can but doesn't
have to be ethnic or racial,

01:00:56.530 --> 01:00:58.610
it can also be political.

01:00:58.610 --> 01:01:02.100
You can politicize any group, in fact,

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:05.260
and make it into an enemy group.

01:01:05.260 --> 01:01:08.840
You don't even need to have
ethnic and racial differences.

01:01:08.840 --> 01:01:10.820
- Okay, if it's all
right I'm gonna go ahead

01:01:10.820 --> 01:01:13.960
and just read some of
these thoughtful questions

01:01:13.960 --> 01:01:14.793
that we have.

01:01:18.853 --> 01:01:20.130
With the convention, by the way,

01:01:20.130 --> 01:01:20.963
to all of you who are watching

01:01:20.963 --> 01:01:22.230
is that I'm not gonna
give the names of people

01:01:22.230 --> 01:01:23.830
who are asking the questions,

01:01:23.830 --> 01:01:25.720
so I'm just gonna give the question.

01:01:25.720 --> 01:01:27.420
So, a question is,

01:01:27.420 --> 01:01:28.970
and I'm also gonna skip
the part where you say

01:01:28.970 --> 01:01:30.470
how great a lecture it is.

01:01:30.470 --> 01:01:31.303
I'm gonna skip that part,

01:01:31.303 --> 01:01:32.977
I'm gonna go right to the question.

01:01:32.977 --> 01:01:34.367
"I was really surprised,"
this is a question,

01:01:34.367 --> 01:01:36.497
"I was really surprised
that more of Russian society

01:01:36.497 --> 01:01:38.157
"doesn't talk about the Gulags

01:01:38.157 --> 01:01:40.687
"given how extensively the
system had impacted the lives

01:01:40.687 --> 01:01:42.407
"of many of the Stalin era.

01:01:42.407 --> 01:01:44.697
"My question is about
public and social memory.

01:01:44.697 --> 01:01:46.167
"I was curious about the differences

01:01:46.167 --> 01:01:49.837
"between Russians who had
experiences with incarceration

01:01:49.837 --> 01:01:51.297
"and those who did not.

01:01:51.297 --> 01:01:54.237
"Could you tell us more about
your conversations with them,

01:01:54.237 --> 01:01:56.557
"that is people who had been incarcerated.

01:01:56.557 --> 01:01:58.987
"How did they remember
the Gulag differently?

01:01:58.987 --> 01:02:00.097
"Did the perception and language

01:02:00.097 --> 01:02:02.057
"of those who had been incarcerated

01:02:02.057 --> 01:02:04.077
"challenge the wider amnesia or denial

01:02:04.077 --> 01:02:05.487
"in wider Russian society

01:02:05.487 --> 01:02:07.787
"or was there a similar
cognitive dissonance?"

01:02:09.840 --> 01:02:14.190
- It's a trickier question to answer now

01:02:14.190 --> 01:02:17.460
than it was really, frankly, 30 years ago,

01:02:17.460 --> 01:02:19.823
25 years ago when I was
working on the book.

01:02:20.830 --> 01:02:22.620
The generation of people who I interviewed

01:02:22.620 --> 01:02:23.580
aren't there anymore,

01:02:23.580 --> 01:02:26.000
and so this isn't a living question

01:02:26.000 --> 01:02:28.470
in the same way that it
was a few decades ago

01:02:28.470 --> 01:02:33.470
because most Gulag survivors
are no longer with us.

01:02:34.260 --> 01:02:37.860
Enough time has passed.

01:02:37.860 --> 01:02:40.470
I mean, it's a question
that's complicated by politics

01:02:40.470 --> 01:02:43.390
because, as I said,

01:02:43.390 --> 01:02:48.010
while the Gulag existed,
everybody knew about it.

01:02:48.010 --> 01:02:50.730
It was a secret and not a secret,

01:02:50.730 --> 01:02:52.370
a little bit in the way
that famine in Ukraine

01:02:52.370 --> 01:02:53.760
was a secret but not a secret.

01:02:53.760 --> 01:02:56.560
So you couldn't not know.

01:02:56.560 --> 01:02:59.460
Everybody lived in communities
where there were prisoners.

01:03:00.340 --> 01:03:03.240
If we're talking about arrest
of 10 or 15% of the population

01:03:03.240 --> 01:03:06.249
then everybody has a cousin
or a friend or a relative,

01:03:06.249 --> 01:03:09.260
everyone knew that
someone had been arrested.

01:03:09.260 --> 01:03:12.030
So the lack of...

01:03:12.030 --> 01:03:13.550
And it raises an interesting question

01:03:13.550 --> 01:03:16.140
about what is knowledge and
when do people know things

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:17.720
and what do we mean by public memory,

01:03:17.720 --> 01:03:19.480
because everyone knew

01:03:19.480 --> 01:03:22.010
and yet it was not allowed to be discussed

01:03:22.010 --> 01:03:25.040
or you weren't able to discuss it.

01:03:25.040 --> 01:03:27.870
And so the way that conversations
about the Gulag worked

01:03:27.870 --> 01:03:30.460
is that in very private little circles

01:03:30.460 --> 01:03:32.030
people did talk about it.

01:03:32.030 --> 01:03:35.020
And so there's a famous
circle that developed

01:03:35.020 --> 01:03:36.250
around Eugenia Ginzburg

01:03:36.250 --> 01:03:40.620
who was a famous Gulag survivor in Moscow

01:03:40.620 --> 01:03:43.440
and she was a sort of proto dissident

01:03:43.440 --> 01:03:45.350
really before there were dissidents,

01:03:45.350 --> 01:03:47.480
and she gathered around herself

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:50.120
a lot of people who would
come and would talk about it,

01:03:50.120 --> 01:03:53.310
and their friends and
relatives would know about it

01:03:53.310 --> 01:03:56.900
and they were able to
speak about it freely,

01:03:56.900 --> 01:03:58.510
and their children would know about it

01:03:58.510 --> 01:04:00.310
and I've met some of their children,

01:04:01.170 --> 01:04:04.920
and people in their
immediate circle would know.

01:04:04.920 --> 01:04:09.510
And then there would be
other people in other circles

01:04:09.510 --> 01:04:10.900
who would never speak about it again.

01:04:10.900 --> 01:04:12.080
They would come home and never talk.

01:04:12.080 --> 01:04:15.580
So actually the real
distinction wasn't between

01:04:15.580 --> 01:04:17.340
people who were there and
people who weren't there.

01:04:17.340 --> 01:04:19.370
The real distinction was between

01:04:19.370 --> 01:04:22.700
people who because of the politics

01:04:22.700 --> 01:04:25.940
and the culture of their
particular community

01:04:25.940 --> 01:04:28.098
were able to speak about
it and those who weren't.

01:04:28.098 --> 01:04:31.380
So in interviewing people you
got very different reactions.

01:04:31.380 --> 01:04:35.020
I had one or two people I
interviewed in the 1990s

01:04:35.020 --> 01:04:37.560
who were still really too
frightened to speak about it.

01:04:37.560 --> 01:04:41.360
They very much didn't wanna say,

01:04:41.360 --> 01:04:43.877
very clammed up, nervous talking to me.

01:04:43.877 --> 01:04:46.623
"What are you gonna do with
this information if I tell you?

01:04:46.623 --> 01:04:49.060
"Why do you have a tape recorder?"

01:04:49.060 --> 01:04:51.660
So even then there were
people who were afraid

01:04:51.660 --> 01:04:52.510
and then there were people

01:04:52.510 --> 01:04:54.110
who you would ask them one question

01:04:54.110 --> 01:04:55.930
and they would speak for hours.

01:04:55.930 --> 01:04:57.988
In fact, sometimes that was a problem,

01:04:57.988 --> 01:05:02.470
as anybody who's ever
done oral history knows.

01:05:02.470 --> 01:05:04.010
You had to kind of get them back

01:05:04.010 --> 01:05:06.940
onto the thing you really
needed them to speak about.

01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:09.380
So the difference was,

01:05:09.380 --> 01:05:13.040
the difference was that there
were pockets of the society

01:05:13.040 --> 01:05:16.240
where it was okay to speak
about these things in private

01:05:16.240 --> 01:05:19.373
and then there were other
pockets where it wasn't.

01:05:20.940 --> 01:05:22.060
And why that was,

01:05:22.060 --> 01:05:25.810
it could be to do with fear,
it could be to do with status,

01:05:25.810 --> 01:05:27.980
the higher status you
were in the Soviet system

01:05:27.980 --> 01:05:31.590
the less you were likely to
wanna discuss these things.

01:05:31.590 --> 01:05:34.000
It could be to do with personalities.

01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:38.427
So the memory of it really
depended on where you were

01:05:38.427 --> 01:05:40.980
and something like that persists today.

01:05:40.980 --> 01:05:45.020
So you can still go to Moscow

01:05:45.020 --> 01:05:48.350
and you can meet people who
know a lot about Russian history

01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:50.750
and will speak about it at great length.

01:05:50.750 --> 01:05:52.590
There are some great Russian historians

01:05:52.590 --> 01:05:53.900
who write in Russian.

01:05:53.900 --> 01:05:56.480
It's not as if there's no
research done on this subject.

01:05:56.480 --> 01:05:59.270
Actually, all the best
historians are Russians.

01:05:59.270 --> 01:06:00.103
The really good ones.

01:06:00.103 --> 01:06:01.830
I think you're reading Oleg Volkov.

01:06:04.563 --> 01:06:07.220
The ones who really know
the most are Russians.

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:09.430
There are a couple of
Russian institutions,

01:06:09.430 --> 01:06:10.460
there are a few publishing houses.

01:06:10.460 --> 01:06:12.553
My Gulag book has been
published in Russia,

01:06:12.553 --> 01:06:16.280
a very, very small, I think
500 copies or something,

01:06:16.280 --> 01:06:18.460
but it was translated.

01:06:18.460 --> 01:06:21.172
So there is a kind of community of people

01:06:21.172 --> 01:06:22.005
who speaks about it,

01:06:22.005 --> 01:06:24.320
it's not totally illegal.

01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:25.660
But what there isn't,

01:06:25.660 --> 01:06:28.563
it's not part of public
discussion or debate.

01:06:30.040 --> 01:06:31.800
I'm guessing that depending on the teacher

01:06:31.800 --> 01:06:34.580
it might occasionally appear
in a school curriculum.

01:06:34.580 --> 01:06:36.220
But there isn't a memorial,

01:06:36.220 --> 01:06:38.220
there isn't an annual celebration

01:06:39.150 --> 01:06:40.570
like there is Holocaust Day

01:06:40.570 --> 01:06:43.060
or Holodomor Day in Ukraine.

01:06:43.060 --> 01:06:44.730
There isn't a...

01:06:46.730 --> 01:06:48.010
There isn't a...

01:06:48.900 --> 01:06:51.280
It's not part of the national narrative.

01:06:51.280 --> 01:06:53.570
Putin rewrote the national narrative

01:06:53.570 --> 01:06:56.310
to focus not on the Gulag, not on Stalin,

01:06:56.310 --> 01:06:59.020
not on the dissidence, not
on the human rights movement,

01:06:59.020 --> 01:07:02.180
but on 1945 which was the year

01:07:02.180 --> 01:07:03.823
of the greatest Soviet
domination of Europe

01:07:03.823 --> 01:07:05.500
and the victory in the war.

01:07:05.500 --> 01:07:07.710
And so every year now
there's an annual parade

01:07:07.710 --> 01:07:12.070
that mimics this victory
parade that they had in 1945.

01:07:12.070 --> 01:07:15.760
And so somehow this story
gets left out of the picture.

01:07:15.760 --> 01:07:19.580
And so, again, it's not
illegal, it's not impossible,

01:07:19.580 --> 01:07:23.040
it's just not present in public culture,

01:07:23.040 --> 01:07:26.150
and public culture is very
heavily controlled by the state.

01:07:26.150 --> 01:07:29.510
So there isn't a culture
of independent universities

01:07:29.510 --> 01:07:32.143
who can argue with the public culture.

01:07:33.958 --> 01:07:37.240
There are independent
magazines and newspapers

01:07:37.240 --> 01:07:39.700
but they're increasingly
oppressed and marginalized,

01:07:39.700 --> 01:07:42.300
quite a lot of them are
based outside of Russia now.

01:07:43.160 --> 01:07:45.960
The journalists have difficulty
making themselves heard.

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:49.360
And actually there's a famous
case going on right now.

01:07:49.360 --> 01:07:51.570
There is a Gulag historian,

01:07:51.570 --> 01:07:53.020
he was a sort of amateur historian

01:07:53.020 --> 01:07:56.189
and archeologist from Karelia

01:07:56.189 --> 01:07:59.505
and who I actually met while
I was working on the book

01:07:59.505 --> 01:08:02.100
and whose family I've been in touch with,

01:08:02.100 --> 01:08:03.780
who's now under arrest.

01:08:03.780 --> 01:08:07.040
And he's under arrest
on some false pretenses

01:08:07.040 --> 01:08:09.530
to do with supposedly child pornography.

01:08:09.530 --> 01:08:11.770
It's a completely trumped out fake case.

01:08:11.770 --> 01:08:13.670
But the real reason he's under arrest

01:08:13.670 --> 01:08:15.770
is because he's a public historian

01:08:15.770 --> 01:08:17.250
who spoke about these issues

01:08:17.250 --> 01:08:18.730
and was very much associated with them

01:08:18.730 --> 01:08:21.533
and Petrozavodsk which is
the town that he comes from.

01:08:22.405 --> 01:08:24.570
So you can be,

01:08:24.570 --> 01:08:26.460
people who are too loud about these things

01:08:26.460 --> 01:08:27.623
can get in trouble.

01:08:28.890 --> 01:08:32.400
Some people continue in private
circles to speak about it.

01:08:32.400 --> 01:08:35.250
But it's not part of what
Putin wants people to think of

01:08:35.250 --> 01:08:37.263
when they think about
the history of Russia.

01:08:38.130 --> 01:08:40.773
- So this question,
this epistemic question,

01:08:41.700 --> 01:08:44.270
as you said, what it means to
actually know about something,

01:08:44.270 --> 01:08:46.510
and how knowledge comes out

01:08:46.510 --> 01:08:48.200
and how it might have a political effect

01:08:48.200 --> 01:08:49.730
is something which is on the minds

01:08:49.730 --> 01:08:53.240
of I think a number of the students.

01:08:53.240 --> 01:08:54.450
You referred indirectly

01:08:54.450 --> 01:08:57.260
to the chronicle of
current events in Russia

01:08:57.260 --> 01:08:59.470
and the attempt by, you know,

01:08:59.470 --> 01:09:00.890
Russians and Ukrainians and others

01:09:00.890 --> 01:09:03.330
just to write down the simplest details

01:09:03.330 --> 01:09:05.620
but abundant details of
what they could learn about-

01:09:05.620 --> 01:09:07.510
- There's a lot about the
chronicle of current events

01:09:07.510 --> 01:09:09.459
in the book.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:09:09.459 --> 01:09:11.500
This is a theme which is gonna emerge

01:09:11.500 --> 01:09:12.333
over the course of the class.

01:09:12.333 --> 01:09:13.670
I have a question which is about it

01:09:13.670 --> 01:09:15.233
so I'm gonna read it to you.

01:09:16.267 --> 01:09:19.407
"You said that the Gulag
is rarely discussed today

01:09:19.407 --> 01:09:20.240
"but that when it was

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:22.757
"the conversation delegitimized
the Soviet regime."

01:09:23.670 --> 01:09:25.220
So now we're gonna move slowly

01:09:25.220 --> 01:09:27.100
into some comparative questions

01:09:27.100 --> 01:09:29.457
'cause this student is
wondering how this works.

01:09:29.457 --> 01:09:32.107
"In the US we've had repeated
societal conversations

01:09:32.107 --> 01:09:33.257
"about mass incarceration,

01:09:33.257 --> 01:09:34.757
"specially in the last year.

01:09:34.757 --> 01:09:35.957
"Do you think this public debate

01:09:35.957 --> 01:09:39.037
"has deligitimized institutions
like prisons genuinely,

01:09:39.037 --> 01:09:40.647
"or superficially, and if the latter,

01:09:40.647 --> 01:09:42.817
"what's missing in the public dialogue?"

01:09:44.890 --> 01:09:48.260
- That I don't know that
I'm competent to answer

01:09:48.260 --> 01:09:49.930
because I haven't spent a lot of time

01:09:49.930 --> 01:09:52.243
thinking about this question in the US.

01:09:53.740 --> 01:09:55.690
But it's certainly true

01:09:55.690 --> 01:09:59.740
that conversation about
mass incarceration has,

01:09:59.740 --> 01:10:01.400
I mean, for example,

01:10:01.400 --> 01:10:03.962
the criminal justice bill passed

01:10:03.962 --> 01:10:06.880
during the Clinton presidency

01:10:06.880 --> 01:10:11.280
which is now held to be
responsible for mass arrests

01:10:12.230 --> 01:10:15.400
was for a long time by a
pretty wide range of people

01:10:15.400 --> 01:10:18.385
thought to be a good piece of legislation.

01:10:18.385 --> 01:10:21.370
It was something that Clinton himself

01:10:21.370 --> 01:10:23.560
would have taken credit for at
the time that it was passed.

01:10:23.560 --> 01:10:26.340
And quite a lot of people
actually black and white

01:10:26.340 --> 01:10:29.270
it had a lot of support.

01:10:29.270 --> 01:10:33.400
Retrospectively, it's
now heavily criticized

01:10:33.400 --> 01:10:36.850
and it's now seen as the
source of these unfair arrests

01:10:36.850 --> 01:10:39.113
or unfair prison sentences.

01:10:40.112 --> 01:10:44.650
And even things like the,

01:10:44.650 --> 01:10:48.050
and in a similar way laws on marihuana

01:10:48.050 --> 01:10:50.350
I think are now retrospectively seen

01:10:50.350 --> 01:10:54.370
as overtly harsh and ridiculously punitive

01:10:54.370 --> 01:10:58.543
given that Americans drink so much anyway.

01:11:00.596 --> 01:11:02.850
So one thing that conversation does

01:11:02.850 --> 01:11:06.370
I wouldn't say that it's
delegitimized the political system

01:11:06.370 --> 01:11:08.760
but it's certainly delegitimized

01:11:08.760 --> 01:11:11.410
the assumptions that led to this process.

01:11:11.410 --> 01:11:13.630
So do we really need these laws?

01:11:13.630 --> 01:11:15.940
Was this the right way to do it?

01:11:15.940 --> 01:11:19.160
Certainly, and maybe Jason
you can speak to this

01:11:19.160 --> 01:11:21.240
more eloquently that I can,

01:11:21.240 --> 01:11:24.640
certainly the conversation about

01:11:24.640 --> 01:11:27.750
police reform, police brutality

01:11:27.750 --> 01:11:30.630
has been bumped up I think and probably

01:11:31.800 --> 01:11:35.060
also given energy by this question of,

01:11:35.060 --> 01:11:36.750
are too many people in prison?

01:11:36.750 --> 01:11:38.540
Have too many people been locked up?

01:11:38.540 --> 01:11:43.030
So, the conversation,

01:11:43.030 --> 01:11:45.230
having this be part of
the public conversation

01:11:45.230 --> 01:11:48.240
does begin to affect the way
that people see the police,

01:11:48.240 --> 01:11:50.870
the way people see the
criminal justice system,

01:11:50.870 --> 01:11:54.273
the way people see these laws,

01:11:55.289 --> 01:11:58.520
laws on, whatever, three
strikes and you're out,

01:11:58.520 --> 01:12:02.630
the various laws that led

01:12:02.630 --> 01:12:05.550
to larger and longer prison sentences.

01:12:05.550 --> 01:12:08.320
So, yes, I think the public
conversation in that sense

01:12:08.320 --> 01:12:12.523
did delegitimize that part of
the criminal justice system.

01:12:13.700 --> 01:12:15.910
But not my area of expertise,

01:12:15.910 --> 01:12:18.843
so I would like to hear
Jason talk about it.

01:12:21.483 --> 01:12:23.420
- I mean, in fairness,

01:12:23.420 --> 01:12:25.492
there are few people who
are capable of talking

01:12:25.492 --> 01:12:27.160
about both of these
things at the same time,

01:12:27.160 --> 01:12:29.330
and that's one of the reasons why

01:12:29.330 --> 01:12:32.220
we're doing this exploratory course.

01:12:32.220 --> 01:12:33.570
The point is not that Jason and I

01:12:33.570 --> 01:12:35.050
know everything about both systems

01:12:35.050 --> 01:12:36.600
and we're gonna lay it out there.

01:12:36.600 --> 01:12:37.890
The point is that we have these two

01:12:37.890 --> 01:12:39.130
really interesting phenomena

01:12:39.130 --> 01:12:43.447
and we're gonna juxtapose and
we're gonna learn as we go-

01:12:43.447 --> 01:12:47.740
- It's like comparing the
Holocaust and the Gulag.

01:12:47.740 --> 01:12:49.659
They're not the same.

01:12:49.659 --> 01:12:52.260
But it's interesting to talk
about them together, so.

01:12:52.260 --> 01:12:53.850
- Yeah, some people
have even written books

01:12:53.850 --> 01:12:55.131
where they're together.

01:12:55.131 --> 01:12:57.110
- I don't know anyone, but
some people might have.

01:12:57.110 --> 01:12:59.510
- There's probably somebody
out there somewhere.

01:13:00.770 --> 01:13:02.804
So, I'm gonna,

01:13:02.804 --> 01:13:04.890
there's an important
substantive question here

01:13:04.890 --> 01:13:06.140
which going back to Russia

01:13:06.140 --> 01:13:09.100
and then maybe we'll do
a little more comparison.

01:13:09.100 --> 01:13:13.180
Was there continuity between
punishment or incarceration

01:13:13.180 --> 01:13:16.050
in czarist Russia before 1917

01:13:16.050 --> 01:13:18.020
through the development of
mass incarceration in Gulag

01:13:18.020 --> 01:13:19.253
in the next decades?

01:13:20.140 --> 01:13:22.770
And, and another student
asks this question as well,

01:13:22.770 --> 01:13:24.430
was there continuity between the Gulag

01:13:24.430 --> 01:13:28.690
and post-Soviet Russia?
- Right.

01:13:28.690 --> 01:13:32.653
So the answer is kind
of yes and kind of yes.

01:13:36.340 --> 01:13:37.640
The czarist system

01:13:39.920 --> 01:13:43.253
was actually, retrospectively,
relatively mild.

01:13:44.190 --> 01:13:47.970
If you look at the numbers
of political prisoners,

01:13:47.970 --> 01:13:50.570
they're in the few
hundreds or few thousands.

01:13:50.570 --> 01:13:53.920
I think at the beginning of the revolution

01:13:53.920 --> 01:13:55.660
there were a few thousands
political prisoners,

01:13:55.660 --> 01:13:58.610
which is by comparison to the millions

01:13:58.610 --> 01:13:59.850
who were imprisoned by Stalin

01:13:59.850 --> 01:14:01.320
is very very low.

01:14:01.320 --> 01:14:02.860
And the system was very loose.

01:14:02.860 --> 01:14:04.540
In fact, it was so famously loose

01:14:04.540 --> 01:14:07.470
that Stalin escaped I think three times

01:14:07.470 --> 01:14:10.740
from Russian places of exile,

01:14:10.740 --> 01:14:13.130
which is sort of amusing
if you think about it.

01:14:13.130 --> 01:14:15.470
And one of his, you know,

01:14:15.470 --> 01:14:17.990
it was so easy to escape
from Russian prisons

01:14:17.990 --> 01:14:19.950
that he decided that when
he was finally in charge

01:14:19.950 --> 01:14:22.940
he was gonna make sure
that they weren't so easy

01:14:22.940 --> 01:14:24.560
to escape from again.

01:14:24.560 --> 01:14:27.313
So there were substantive
differences in scale,

01:14:28.360 --> 01:14:29.490
but there were a few things,

01:14:29.490 --> 01:14:33.000
so the idea of sending prisoners

01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:35.473
to the Far North and the Far East,

01:14:36.355 --> 01:14:39.250
this was a czarist idea.

01:14:39.250 --> 01:14:42.780
The idea that prisoners
should somehow be used

01:14:42.780 --> 01:14:45.130
to exploit these coal mines

01:14:45.130 --> 01:14:49.620
or gold mines in the distant tundra,

01:14:49.620 --> 01:14:51.080
this was also a czarist idea.

01:14:51.080 --> 01:14:52.500
So the czars also had the idea

01:14:52.500 --> 01:14:53.690
that you could use prisoners

01:14:53.690 --> 01:14:56.950
for these things that were
very difficult to achieve

01:14:56.950 --> 01:14:59.470
using normal people.

01:14:59.470 --> 01:15:02.010
And so this was something
that the Bolsheviks picked up,

01:15:02.010 --> 01:15:03.723
this was an element of continuity.

01:15:04.960 --> 01:15:07.250
Also, the other element
of continuity was the idea

01:15:07.250 --> 01:15:11.273
that you could send prisoners
to settle empty places.

01:15:11.273 --> 01:15:14.320
That there was all this empty
space in the Soviet Union

01:15:14.320 --> 01:15:17.700
and we need to somehow fill it
and nobody wants to go there

01:15:17.700 --> 01:15:20.140
'cause there's too many
mosquitoes and too cold,

01:15:20.140 --> 01:15:22.430
so we can send prisoners there to do it.

01:15:22.430 --> 01:15:25.500
And that idea is continuous
all the way through.

01:15:25.500 --> 01:15:28.000
And some of the places, I mean,

01:15:28.000 --> 01:15:31.900
some of the places of exile
in the Russian czarist era

01:15:31.900 --> 01:15:34.270
and the Soviet era are the same.

01:15:34.270 --> 01:15:38.210
And so there is a kind of
continuity of thinking about this.

01:15:38.210 --> 01:15:39.790
Stalin didn't make up the idea

01:15:39.790 --> 01:15:43.080
that you could use prisoners
to dig coal in the Far North.

01:15:43.080 --> 01:15:44.890
That was an older idea.

01:15:44.890 --> 01:15:48.050
And then I would say that
there is a similarity

01:15:48.050 --> 01:15:50.910
not of the prisons but of the systems

01:15:50.910 --> 01:15:53.425
in that the lack of rule of law,

01:15:53.425 --> 01:15:57.940
the fact that the ruler
could decide what the law was

01:15:57.940 --> 01:16:01.280
and what the sentence
was at any given moment

01:16:01.280 --> 01:16:02.570
and that that could change

01:16:02.570 --> 01:16:04.460
depending on political circumstances,

01:16:04.460 --> 01:16:07.380
this is continuous between czarist Russia

01:16:07.380 --> 01:16:08.240
and Stalin's Russia.

01:16:08.240 --> 01:16:11.994
So there's no independent court system,

01:16:11.994 --> 01:16:14.480
there's no independent way of making law

01:16:14.480 --> 01:16:16.944
or of overseeing prison sentences.

01:16:16.944 --> 01:16:20.560
The czar suddenly decides
that this group of people,

01:16:20.560 --> 01:16:23.230
in the case of the Decembrists uprising,

01:16:23.230 --> 01:16:26.190
this group of people deserve
specially hard sentences,

01:16:26.190 --> 01:16:28.300
and he could decide that
they would get them.

01:16:28.300 --> 01:16:31.395
And that's a similarity
to the Soviet system,

01:16:31.395 --> 01:16:34.930
and then that's an element of continuity

01:16:34.930 --> 01:16:35.930
that you have today.

01:16:36.830 --> 01:16:41.510
In the Soviet Union I would
say in the 90s and early 2000s

01:16:41.510 --> 01:16:43.920
it was fair to say the
Gulag system had ended,

01:16:43.920 --> 01:16:46.573
there wasn't a system of
political prisons in Russia.

01:16:47.770 --> 01:16:50.920
There is now beginning to be that again.

01:16:50.920 --> 01:16:52.280
There are beginning to be people

01:16:52.280 --> 01:16:54.950
who are sentenced for
purely political crimes

01:16:54.950 --> 01:16:57.300
and are put in,

01:16:57.300 --> 01:16:59.840
they're put in, you know,

01:16:59.840 --> 01:17:01.347
there are still prison camps,

01:17:01.347 --> 01:17:04.140
and very often they're in the same place

01:17:04.140 --> 01:17:05.140
as the Soviet camps.

01:17:05.140 --> 01:17:08.650
I was once in a prison in
Arkhangelsk, of all places.

01:17:08.650 --> 01:17:11.970
This is, again, this is about
early 2000s, I would say.

01:17:11.970 --> 01:17:14.450
I was in the prison, it was
a modern Russian prison.

01:17:14.450 --> 01:17:15.283
This was not a political prison,

01:17:15.283 --> 01:17:17.090
these were just criminal prisoners.

01:17:17.090 --> 01:17:20.550
And it was the same
building that had been used

01:17:20.550 --> 01:17:22.200
during the Soviet period

01:17:22.200 --> 01:17:24.220
and it was the same
building that had been used

01:17:24.220 --> 01:17:25.580
during the czarist period.

01:17:25.580 --> 01:17:28.330
So very often the places and
the buildings are the same,

01:17:28.330 --> 01:17:31.580
and then of course that also
means that institutionally

01:17:31.580 --> 01:17:33.920
the behavior of the guards, the customs,

01:17:33.920 --> 01:17:36.180
how people are treated,

01:17:36.180 --> 01:17:38.670
there's a kind of constant
institutional memory,

01:17:38.670 --> 01:17:40.830
and that's true of the present.

01:17:40.830 --> 01:17:42.819
If you read Mikhail Khodorkovsky

01:17:42.819 --> 01:17:44.100
who was a Russian businessman

01:17:44.100 --> 01:17:45.860
who was in prison for many years,

01:17:45.860 --> 01:17:48.060
if you read his memoirs he talks about

01:17:48.060 --> 01:17:50.900
being treated by guards in
ways that sound very familiar

01:17:50.900 --> 01:17:53.250
to anybody who's read
the history of the Gulag.

01:17:54.490 --> 01:17:56.990
So although the numbers aren't as great,

01:17:56.990 --> 01:17:58.960
although they're building up again slowly,

01:17:58.960 --> 01:18:01.010
the numbers of purely political prisoners

01:18:02.110 --> 01:18:03.390
aren't as large.

01:18:03.390 --> 01:18:05.000
The prisons themselves,

01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:08.190
the systems by which
they're run and organized,

01:18:08.190 --> 01:18:10.600
the language and nature of the guards,

01:18:10.600 --> 01:18:13.920
the conditions are surprisingly,

01:18:13.920 --> 01:18:16.610
there are surprising
similarities and echoes

01:18:16.610 --> 01:18:17.480
up until the present.

01:18:17.480 --> 01:18:21.090
So it's almost as if you
can change the politics

01:18:21.090 --> 01:18:23.373
but you don't necessarily
change the jailers.

01:18:24.900 --> 01:18:28.030
The jailers still, when
someone goes to work in a jail,

01:18:28.030 --> 01:18:29.680
he's going to work for an older person

01:18:29.680 --> 01:18:31.350
who used to work in the jail.

01:18:31.350 --> 01:18:34.280
And if you haven't had a
major prison revolution,

01:18:34.280 --> 01:18:36.360
or prison reform I mean, sorry,

01:18:36.360 --> 01:18:37.870
which you have not had in Russia,

01:18:37.870 --> 01:18:40.940
you did not have it after the 1990s,

01:18:40.940 --> 01:18:43.920
then why would they change?

01:18:43.920 --> 01:18:46.010
The system remains the system

01:18:46.010 --> 01:18:47.657
as it was invented many years ago.

01:18:47.657 --> 01:18:50.507
It has been altered, people
aren't starved to death anymore

01:18:50.507 --> 01:18:52.120
and they aren't worked to death anymore,

01:18:52.120 --> 01:18:56.091
but the system as it was in the 80s

01:18:56.091 --> 01:18:57.340
and the system as it is now

01:18:57.340 --> 01:18:58.610
aren't remarkably different,

01:18:58.610 --> 01:19:02.110
which isn't surprising given
that the chain of people,

01:19:02.110 --> 01:19:04.850
the chain of, you know,

01:19:04.850 --> 01:19:08.030
the training and the
experience of the guards

01:19:08.030 --> 01:19:09.890
is very similar.

01:19:09.890 --> 01:19:12.113
- Can I ask a quick question, Tim?

01:19:12.113 --> 01:19:15.870
So, we discussed Foucault's
"Discipline and Punish"

01:19:15.870 --> 01:19:17.140
last week in class,

01:19:17.140 --> 01:19:19.550
and Foucault has this
discussion of illegality

01:19:19.550 --> 01:19:21.280
where he says it's important to create

01:19:21.280 --> 01:19:22.990
this zone of illegality

01:19:22.990 --> 01:19:24.690
so that revolute people who,

01:19:24.690 --> 01:19:28.239
the class that might
rebel against the system

01:19:28.239 --> 01:19:31.530
is thrown into prison as criminals.

01:19:31.530 --> 01:19:34.680
They're delegitimized
as political prisoners.

01:19:34.680 --> 01:19:37.030
So, one, when they do seek to rebel,

01:19:37.030 --> 01:19:39.080
you say oh they're just common criminals.

01:19:40.650 --> 01:19:43.253
That's obviously a theme
in the United States.

01:19:44.099 --> 01:19:45.360
To what extent is that a theme-

01:19:45.360 --> 01:19:47.210
- Huge, really important theme

01:19:47.210 --> 01:19:49.910
both in Stalin's Russia
and in modern Russia.

01:19:49.910 --> 01:19:52.030
In Stalin's Russia there were so many laws

01:19:52.030 --> 01:19:54.090
and there were so many ways to break them

01:19:54.090 --> 01:19:56.400
that it was very hard
not to break any laws.

01:19:56.400 --> 01:19:58.910
But even in modern Russia
there's an excellent line,

01:19:58.910 --> 01:20:01.510
there's a book by Chrystia Freeland

01:20:01.510 --> 01:20:02.980
who's now a Canadian politician

01:20:02.980 --> 01:20:03.970
but who used to be a journalist,

01:20:03.970 --> 01:20:05.190
wrote a very good book about Russia,

01:20:05.190 --> 01:20:08.170
it was about the
privatization era of the 90s,

01:20:08.170 --> 01:20:11.270
and she had a line in
it where she talks about

01:20:11.270 --> 01:20:13.780
Western advisors working in Russia

01:20:13.780 --> 01:20:15.587
constantly advising
the Russians, they say,

01:20:15.587 --> 01:20:17.877
"Well, you have all these
contradictory laws in which,

01:20:17.877 --> 01:20:20.927
"you know, people doing
business in this, you know,

01:20:20.927 --> 01:20:24.560
"are always breaking the law
because the laws are unclear."

01:20:24.560 --> 01:20:26.247
And she said, "We kept pushing this.

01:20:26.247 --> 01:20:27.427
"This is a big problem for you,

01:20:27.427 --> 01:20:28.270
"why don't you fix this problem?"

01:20:28.270 --> 01:20:32.970
And then suddenly it finally
dawns on them it's on purpose.

01:20:32.970 --> 01:20:35.990
The laws are unclear for a reason.

01:20:35.990 --> 01:20:38.440
Because everybody is a criminal.

01:20:38.440 --> 01:20:41.459
So anybody can be arrested at any moment

01:20:41.459 --> 01:20:45.170
if the state doesn't want them to be,

01:20:45.170 --> 01:20:46.440
if they don't like their business

01:20:46.440 --> 01:20:48.810
or they're not paying enough
money to the mafia boss

01:20:48.810 --> 01:20:50.350
or whatever it is that they're doing,

01:20:50.350 --> 01:20:52.810
they've always violated something

01:20:52.810 --> 01:20:54.170
because the laws are badly written

01:20:54.170 --> 01:20:55.810
and they seem to be written
that way on purpose.

01:20:55.810 --> 01:20:57.403
And so, yes, absolutely,

01:20:58.722 --> 01:21:01.180
the unclear laws, the badly written laws,

01:21:01.180 --> 01:21:04.040
the creation of criminalizing.

01:21:04.040 --> 01:21:05.720
In Stalin's Soviet Union there's a moment

01:21:05.720 --> 01:21:07.053
where they criminalize,

01:21:10.520 --> 01:21:12.820
the theft of grain becomes,

01:21:12.820 --> 01:21:14.670
this is in a moment of heavy starvation,

01:21:14.670 --> 01:21:17.870
becomes such a widely
defined category of behavior

01:21:17.870 --> 01:21:19.790
that you could be arrested for picking it,

01:21:19.790 --> 01:21:22.190
if somebody is carrying a bail of grain

01:21:22.190 --> 01:21:25.000
and you pick up the leftovers
in put them in your pocket,

01:21:25.000 --> 01:21:28.080
that can be a crime of
theft from the Soviet state

01:21:28.080 --> 01:21:30.271
and you can go to prison for five years.

01:21:30.271 --> 01:21:33.688
So all these systems have that phenomenon,

01:21:33.688 --> 01:21:36.790
that many ordinary
activities are criminalized

01:21:36.790 --> 01:21:38.400
or the laws are so badly written

01:21:38.400 --> 01:21:42.053
that you're always bound to be
breaking one of them, so yes.

01:21:43.019 --> 01:21:46.450
- Or the structures are such
that you have to break a law.

01:21:46.450 --> 01:21:48.620
- You have to break a law in
order to do business in Russia.

01:21:48.620 --> 01:21:51.450
You just can't do business
without breaking a law.

01:21:51.450 --> 01:21:53.720
- Yeah, one interesting difference

01:21:54.900 --> 01:21:57.090
between the US and Russia I think,

01:21:57.090 --> 01:21:58.450
people can correct me,

01:21:58.450 --> 01:22:00.170
is that in Russia

01:22:02.096 --> 01:22:04.696
middle class people who are
running small businesses

01:22:05.770 --> 01:22:07.740
are constantly afraid that
they're gonna be arrested

01:22:07.740 --> 01:22:10.180
because they will be, it's
very likely that they will be.

01:22:10.180 --> 01:22:12.260
- If someone doesn't like them

01:22:12.260 --> 01:22:14.261
or wants their business, yeah.

01:22:14.261 --> 01:22:15.210
- Yeah, and in the US,

01:22:15.210 --> 01:22:18.200
at least if you're a white
middle class businessman,

01:22:18.200 --> 01:22:20.570
this is not something
you're thinking about it.

01:22:20.570 --> 01:22:21.880
The laws may be contradictory

01:22:21.880 --> 01:22:24.380
but you're not particularly
worried about this.

01:22:24.380 --> 01:22:26.930
So you can have the same set of friends

01:22:26.930 --> 01:22:31.740
in Cincinnati as you
do in Perm or whatever

01:22:31.740 --> 01:22:33.300
and they're gonna have
very different fears

01:22:33.300 --> 01:22:35.273
about the police coming for them.

01:22:36.755 --> 01:22:38.800
- Yeah, no, I would say,

01:22:38.800 --> 01:22:42.941
in the US there are various recourses.

01:22:42.941 --> 01:22:44.913
You can get lawyers.

01:22:46.571 --> 01:22:47.570
But you're right,

01:22:47.570 --> 01:22:51.070
the ordinary business people
just for doesn't their jobs

01:22:51.070 --> 01:22:52.950
are not afraid of being arrested.

01:22:52.950 --> 01:22:55.020
- Yeah, okay.

01:22:55.020 --> 01:22:57.430
And so who is afraid of that arbitrariness

01:22:57.430 --> 01:22:59.060
is obviously one of the
things we're thinking about.

01:22:59.060 --> 01:23:00.570
You've been very generous,

01:23:00.570 --> 01:23:04.860
I'm gonna give you two
questions together about the US

01:23:04.860 --> 01:23:07.210
knowing that this is not what
you really wanna talk about

01:23:07.210 --> 01:23:08.930
but also since you've kindly volunteered

01:23:08.930 --> 01:23:11.240
to take part in the class
on mass incarceration

01:23:11.240 --> 01:23:12.600
in the US and the Soviet Union

01:23:12.600 --> 01:23:14.180
and then one more question about Russia

01:23:14.180 --> 01:23:16.140
and then we'll thank you again.

01:23:16.140 --> 01:23:19.000
So, it's a general question which is,

01:23:19.000 --> 01:23:20.660
how have your studies of the Gulag

01:23:20.660 --> 01:23:25.370
impacted your understanding of
mass incarceration in the US?

01:23:25.370 --> 01:23:27.290
And then there's a more specific

01:23:27.290 --> 01:23:30.440
social and economic
history question which is,

01:23:30.440 --> 01:23:34.030
if we link labor control and race,

01:23:34.030 --> 01:23:36.380
do you see common threads
between Gulag camps

01:23:36.380 --> 01:23:38.350
and Southern US penitentiaries

01:23:38.350 --> 01:23:40.513
that were specifically plantations?

01:23:41.580 --> 01:23:44.740
Angola, Louisiana,
Parchman Farm, Mississippi,

01:23:44.740 --> 01:23:47.963
and the Texas prison
plantation system, for example.

01:23:50.940 --> 01:23:52.393
- So, the first question,

01:23:53.310 --> 01:23:56.090
I don't know if my study
of the Gulag has impacted

01:23:57.160 --> 01:23:59.833
my understanding of mass
incarceration in the US.

01:24:00.720 --> 01:24:02.610
Certainly a study of the Gulag

01:24:02.610 --> 01:24:05.740
and more broadly a study
of the Soviet Union

01:24:05.740 --> 01:24:08.020
has impacted my understanding

01:24:08.020 --> 01:24:10.613
of how propaganda is used, for example,

01:24:11.580 --> 01:24:13.760
in the US and Europe

01:24:13.760 --> 01:24:16.860
and really everywhere else in the world.

01:24:16.860 --> 01:24:21.860
And it's made me hyper-allergic and aware

01:24:23.160 --> 01:24:26.513
of certain kinds of tactics and
techniques that I, you know,

01:24:27.350 --> 01:24:30.510
again, I don't wanna say
that Stalinism is the same

01:24:30.510 --> 01:24:32.600
as anything we have in the modern world,

01:24:32.600 --> 01:24:34.390
but there are themes.

01:24:34.390 --> 01:24:36.790
I mean, it was while I was
writing my book about Ukraine,

01:24:36.790 --> 01:24:38.880
about the Ukrainian famine,

01:24:38.880 --> 01:24:40.413
I realized sort of,

01:24:41.550 --> 01:24:43.730
Tim and Jason you've probably
had this experience as well,

01:24:43.730 --> 01:24:45.190
you get to the end of the book

01:24:45.190 --> 01:24:47.670
and you suddenly realize what it's about.

01:24:47.670 --> 01:24:49.350
And this is what happened to me

01:24:49.350 --> 01:24:50.310
when I wrote the Ukraine book.

01:24:50.310 --> 01:24:52.660
I got to the end of the
book and I suddenly realized

01:24:52.660 --> 01:24:55.250
it's about dehumanization and hate speech,

01:24:55.250 --> 01:24:57.890
which wasn't how I started
out thinking about the book,

01:24:57.890 --> 01:25:00.630
but of course why was it possible
to starve the Ukrainians?

01:25:00.630 --> 01:25:05.630
It was because they were
publicly vilified in this way.

01:25:05.779 --> 01:25:09.220
And so I would say that my
study of the Soviet Union

01:25:09.220 --> 01:25:13.060
has helped me to think about
propaganda and how it's used

01:25:13.060 --> 01:25:15.950
rather than given me specific insights

01:25:15.950 --> 01:25:17.400
into the American prison system.

01:25:17.400 --> 01:25:19.503
I would put it that way.

01:25:21.840 --> 01:25:23.670
Gosh, is the US,

01:25:23.670 --> 01:25:26.480
I mean, the use of forced labor...

01:25:28.660 --> 01:25:32.710
There is a big argument in
the world of Gulag studies

01:25:32.710 --> 01:25:35.730
as to whether the Gulag counts
as a slave system or not

01:25:35.730 --> 01:25:37.390
or whether it's something different,

01:25:37.390 --> 01:25:40.260
and that's often a kind
of semantic argument

01:25:40.260 --> 01:25:42.927
about how are these things similar

01:25:42.927 --> 01:25:44.277
and how are they different.

01:25:45.520 --> 01:25:50.520
But I would say that the use
of forced labor and slavery

01:25:51.160 --> 01:25:54.453
does have some common features.

01:25:56.601 --> 01:25:58.680
Again, it has to be accompanied

01:25:58.680 --> 01:26:02.270
by a sense of the people who
are doing the forced labor

01:26:02.270 --> 01:26:04.980
aren't of the group as us.

01:26:04.980 --> 01:26:07.041
They have some feature,

01:26:07.041 --> 01:26:09.440
it can be political as it was in Russia,

01:26:09.440 --> 01:26:11.560
it can be ethnic as it was in Russia,

01:26:11.560 --> 01:26:15.150
or it can be racial as it
was in the United States.

01:26:15.150 --> 01:26:17.920
They have to be somehow qualified

01:26:17.920 --> 01:26:20.850
as not deserving of having free will

01:26:20.850 --> 01:26:23.650
and the ability to choose
what kind of labor they do.

01:26:23.650 --> 01:26:26.643
And so you can say that that was the same.

01:26:28.240 --> 01:26:31.589
And the creation of special
categories of people

01:26:31.589 --> 01:26:34.760
who don't deserve citizenship

01:26:34.760 --> 01:26:37.780
and who don't deserve the
protection of the rule of law.

01:26:37.780 --> 01:26:41.320
I mean, the enormous difference
is that there is protection

01:26:41.320 --> 01:26:44.860
for the rule of law for some
people in the United States,

01:26:44.860 --> 01:26:47.020
which there isn't in Russia at all.

01:26:47.020 --> 01:26:52.020
And so it's not exactly
the same kind of problem.

01:26:53.750 --> 01:26:57.560
But yeah I think you
can make that comparison

01:26:57.560 --> 01:26:58.583
when you look at,

01:26:59.450 --> 01:27:02.150
read carefully, this is in my book,

01:27:02.150 --> 01:27:03.360
if you're reading part of the book,

01:27:03.360 --> 01:27:05.236
if you read the chapiter on guards,

01:27:05.236 --> 01:27:10.030
read the justifications that
the guards make to themselves.

01:27:10.030 --> 01:27:12.253
Why is it okay to mistreat prisoners.

01:27:13.510 --> 01:27:15.530
That's somewhere, I'm
afraid I can't tell you

01:27:15.530 --> 01:27:17.980
which page or which chapter
number it is anymore.

01:27:19.070 --> 01:27:21.810
How is it that the guards tell themselves,

01:27:21.810 --> 01:27:24.260
what do they tell themselves
about the prisoners?

01:27:25.297 --> 01:27:28.310
And then you might have an
interesting basis for comparison

01:27:28.310 --> 01:27:29.650
to slave owners.

01:27:29.650 --> 01:27:30.970
What are they telling themselves?

01:27:30.970 --> 01:27:33.800
How are they justifying it to themselves?

01:27:33.800 --> 01:27:35.050
That would be an interesting place

01:27:35.050 --> 01:27:37.570
to look at what's the language being used.

01:27:37.570 --> 01:27:40.670
- Okay, thank you, thank
you for stretching yourself

01:27:40.670 --> 01:27:41.760
in our direction.

01:27:41.760 --> 01:27:43.770
I'm now going to give you a softball

01:27:43.770 --> 01:27:45.553
Soviet historiography question.

01:27:46.850 --> 01:27:48.913
This is from another student.

01:27:50.210 --> 01:27:54.170
If the reason for the Gulag
was fundamentally economic,

01:27:54.170 --> 01:27:56.750
was the stated reason for imprisonment

01:27:56.750 --> 01:27:58.840
being an enemy of the people

01:27:58.840 --> 01:28:01.903
simply a cover for the
economic imperative?

01:28:03.040 --> 01:28:07.380
- Actually, no, maybe I didn't
explain myself that well.

01:28:07.380 --> 01:28:09.190
The stated reason was economic

01:28:09.190 --> 01:28:12.170
and when they talked about themselves,

01:28:12.170 --> 01:28:14.450
when the Stalin and the Yezhov

01:28:14.450 --> 01:28:16.240
or whoever at the politburo

01:28:16.240 --> 01:28:17.490
were talking among themselves

01:28:17.490 --> 01:28:20.290
they spoke about the
Gulag in economic terms.

01:28:20.290 --> 01:28:22.380
But that wasn't really its only reason.

01:28:22.380 --> 01:28:24.503
It was also a form of terror.

01:28:25.382 --> 01:28:28.060
And so it had these dual functions.

01:28:28.060 --> 01:28:30.870
But I found the way they
spoke about it interesting

01:28:30.870 --> 01:28:33.140
for the reason I just answered,

01:28:33.140 --> 01:28:35.860
namely to justify it to themselves,

01:28:35.860 --> 01:28:36.930
to explain what they were doing.

01:28:36.930 --> 01:28:39.957
Remember, these are
people who say in public,

01:28:39.957 --> 01:28:41.437
"We are internationalists,

01:28:41.437 --> 01:28:43.177
"we believe in the
brotherhood of all peoples,

01:28:43.177 --> 01:28:45.427
"we're building the perfect society,

01:28:45.427 --> 01:28:48.050
"we believe in peace and brotherhood."

01:28:48.050 --> 01:28:50.290
They had to explain why this state

01:28:50.290 --> 01:28:54.850
which was extensively about
creating good for mankind,

01:28:54.850 --> 01:28:57.050
why did it need to arrest
millions of people.

01:28:57.050 --> 01:29:01.850
And the reason they gave to
themselves was often economic

01:29:03.472 --> 01:29:05.317
when they spoke among themselves.

01:29:05.317 --> 01:29:08.283
But that doesn't mean it was
the only function of the Gulag.

01:29:08.283 --> 01:29:11.870
I mean, how did it
function in Soviet society?

01:29:11.870 --> 01:29:14.050
And this you can get not
just in prisoners memoirs

01:29:14.050 --> 01:29:16.990
but from other memoirs and
from literature as well.

01:29:16.990 --> 01:29:18.899
It functioned in Soviet society

01:29:18.899 --> 01:29:21.010
as a form of terror,

01:29:21.010 --> 01:29:22.690
so people were afraid of it.

01:29:22.690 --> 01:29:24.840
If I do something wrong,
I'll end up there.

01:29:27.408 --> 01:29:31.845
If I step out of line, my
daughter will be arrested.

01:29:31.845 --> 01:29:35.650
So it functioned as a thing
in the back of people's heads

01:29:35.650 --> 01:29:37.030
that kept people in line,

01:29:37.030 --> 01:29:39.973
so it actually had a number
of different functions.

01:29:41.330 --> 01:29:44.823
And the reasons for arrest, I mean, again,

01:29:47.130 --> 01:29:50.460
there's a famous, Anna
Akhmatova, a famous famous poet,

01:29:50.460 --> 01:29:52.630
who used to say when
people would get arrested

01:29:52.630 --> 01:29:53.560
or someone would be arrested

01:29:53.560 --> 01:29:54.657
and people would try to justify it,

01:29:54.657 --> 01:29:57.847
"Oh, well, so and so, we
knew he would be arrested

01:29:57.847 --> 01:29:59.420
"because he said X or Y."

01:29:59.420 --> 01:30:03.127
She would say, "Don't
you realize everyone,

01:30:03.127 --> 01:30:06.380
"you're all arrested for nothing."

01:30:06.380 --> 01:30:09.943
You could invent any reason
to arrest anybody, anybody.

01:30:11.170 --> 01:30:14.573
And this is another related subject,

01:30:15.430 --> 01:30:20.110
the danger of anonymized
irresponsible bureaucracies

01:30:20.110 --> 01:30:22.170
is that people could use them.

01:30:22.170 --> 01:30:25.410
So you were jealous of
your neighbor's apartment

01:30:25.410 --> 01:30:28.348
and you wanted to get one
of those rooms for yourself,

01:30:28.348 --> 01:30:30.680
that's how housing worked
in the Soviet Union,

01:30:30.680 --> 01:30:32.987
you could denounce your neighbors and say,

01:30:32.987 --> 01:30:37.060
"I heard them saying
something mean about Stalin."

01:30:37.060 --> 01:30:38.672
People would denounce other people

01:30:38.672 --> 01:30:40.880
because they were in love with their wives

01:30:40.880 --> 01:30:42.533
and they wanted to...

01:30:42.533 --> 01:30:46.800
So the arrest system was
also used and manipulated

01:30:48.300 --> 01:30:49.550
by ordinary people

01:30:49.550 --> 01:30:54.510
in order to take advantage of it.

01:30:54.510 --> 01:30:58.250
So, why you were arrested?

01:30:58.250 --> 01:31:00.000
There could be a reason for it,

01:31:00.000 --> 01:31:01.660
you could belong to the wrong ethnic group

01:31:01.660 --> 01:31:03.020
or be at the wrong
place at the wrong time,

01:31:03.020 --> 01:31:06.623
or it could really be
pretty arbitrary and random.

01:31:08.290 --> 01:31:09.820
- Okay.

01:31:09.820 --> 01:31:11.334
We haven't gotten
through all the questions

01:31:11.334 --> 01:31:12.790
but we've gotten through
most of the themes

01:31:12.790 --> 01:31:13.623
of the questions.

01:31:13.623 --> 01:31:14.980
You've been very patient with us

01:31:14.980 --> 01:31:16.600
and we're grateful for that,

01:31:16.600 --> 01:31:20.636
as well as for the accessible, articulate,

01:31:20.636 --> 01:31:22.712
and extremely helpful for us

01:31:22.712 --> 01:31:23.930
at the beginning of our class lecture.

01:31:23.930 --> 01:31:27.660
So, Anne, thank you very
much for being with us.

01:31:27.660 --> 01:31:29.410
Thank you very much for your work

01:31:29.410 --> 01:31:31.513
and thank you for helping us make a start.

01:31:32.353 --> 01:31:35.330
- Thanks to both of you
and thanks to the students.

01:31:35.330 --> 01:31:36.940
It sounds like a fascinating class

01:31:36.940 --> 01:31:38.690
and I'm just sorry I can't take it.

